{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UbMepucU2HA5DSKgvPinxm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L2/043 Mamba-2 Chunk Scan with Segment Sum\n2\t\n3\tTask: optimize the official SOL-ExecBench task `L2/043_mamba_chunk_scan_with_segsum`\n4\ton NVIDIA **A800 (sm_80)**. Primary implementation must be **Triton**; PyTorch only\n5\tfor metadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n6\tRanking: geometric-mean speedup vs. the reference, subject to every selected workload\n7\tpassing correctness.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. Operation summary\n12\t\n13\tThis is the standard **Mamba-2 SSD (State-Space Duality) chunked parallel scan**. The\n14\tsequence is split into chunks of `chunk_size = 256`; within each chunk an\n15\tattention-like \"diagonal\" block is computed, and across chunks a state recurrence with\n16\texponential decay is run. Output = intra-chunk (diagonal) + inter-chunk (off-diagonal)\n17\t+ `D` skip connection.\n18\t\n19\t### Fixed dimensions (from `task/definition.json`)\n20\t| symbol | name | value |\n21\t|--------|------|-------|\n22\t| H | num_heads | 16 (const) |\n23\t| P | head_dim | 64 (const) |\n24\t| N | state_size | 256 (const) |\n25\t| G_grp | n_groups | 1 (const) |\n26\t| Q | chunk_size | 256 (const) |\n27\t| B | batch_size | var |\n28\t| L | seq_len | var |\n29\t| — | hidden_out | H·P = 1024 |\n30\t\n31\tNote `N == Q == 256`, `P == 64`. Everything is bf16 on input/output; the reference does\n32\tall math in **float32** and casts back to bf16 at the very end.\n33\t\n34\t### Inputs / outputs\n35\t- `hidden_states` (X): `[B, L, H, P]` bf16 — the \"values\".\n36\t- `A`: `[B, H, L]` bf16 — per-head log-decay (note the H,L order, transposed vs X).\n37\t- `B` param: `[B, L, G_grp=1, N]` bf16 — shared across heads.\n38\t- `C` param: `[B, L, G_grp=1, N]` bf16 — shared across heads.\n39\t- `D`: `[H]` bf16 — skip/residual scale.\n40\t- `initial_states`: `[B, H, P, N]` bf16 — carry-in SSM state.\n41\t- Output `output`: `[B, L, H·P]` bf16.\n42\t- Output `final_state`: `[B, H, P, N]` bf16.\n43\t\n44\t### Padding / chunking\n45\t`pad = (Q - L % Q) % Q`; seq is zero-padded to `L_pad = L + pad`, `NC = L_pad / Q`\n46\tchunks. Padding value 0 flows through cleanly: A-pad=0 keeps cumsum flat, X-pad=0/B-pad=0\n47\tcontribute nothing, and the tail is cropped back to `L` at the end. So partial chunks need\n48\tonly **row masking on loads (other=0)**, no special-case math.\n49\t\n50\t### Per-workload metadata (derived)\n51\t| B | L | pad | L_pad | NC | last-chunk valid rows | tiles (B·NC·H) |\n52\t|---|-----|-----|-------|----|----------------------|----------------|\n53\t| 2 | 293 | 219 | 512 | 2 | 37 | 64 |\n54\t| 4 | 1024| 0   | 1024 | 4 | 256 | 256 |\n55\t| 4 | 256 | 0   | 256  | 1 | 256 | 64 |\n56\t| 1 | 1024| 0   | 1024 | 4 | 256 | 64 |\n57\t| 4 | 541 | 227 | 768  | 3 | 29 | 192 |\n58\t\n59\tParallel work (independent `(b, chunk, head)` tiles) ranges 64–256. A800 has 108 SMs, so\n60\tfor the 64-tile cases occupancy is a first-order concern — the kernel decomposition should\n61\texpose extra parallelism (e.g. split over head_dim / state, or map heads×batch×chunk to a\n62\tlarge grid) rather than one heavy block per tile.\n63\t\n64\t---\n65\t\n66\t## 2. Algebraic structure (what we actually must compute)\n67\t\n68\tLet, per `(b, chunk, head)`:\n69\t- `a[t] = cumsum_t A[t]` over the chunk (t = 0..Q-1), fp32. `a_last = a[Q-1]`.\n70\t\n71\tThe reference's `segment_sum` is exactly the pairwise cumulative difference:\n72\t`segsum(A)[i,j] = Σ_{k=j+1..i} A[k] = a[i] - a[j]` for `i ≥ j`, else `-inf`.\n73\tTherefore the intra-chunk decay matrix is\n74\t```\n75\tL[i,j] = exp(a[i] - a[j])   for i ≥ j,   0 otherwise   (lower-triangular)\n76\t```\n77\tThis is the crucial simplification: the reference materializes `L` as\n78\t`[B,H,NC,Q,Q]` and `G,M` as `[B,NC,Q,Q,H]` (multi-GB tensors — see §5), but we never\n79\tneed to materialize the `[Q,Q]` (let alone `[Q,Q,H]`) matrices to global memory.\n80\t\n81\t**(1) Intra-chunk / diagonal output**\n82\t- `G[i,j] = C_i · B_j = Σ_s C[i,s] B[j,s]` — **head-independent** because n_groups=1\n83\t  (B, C are broadcast from the single group across all 16 heads). So `G` is computed\n84\t  **once per `(b, chunk)`** and reused by all H heads → up to 16× less CB^T matmul work.\n85\t- `M[i,j] = G[i,j] · L[i,j]` (lower-tri; head-dependent via L).\n86\t- `Y_diag[i,:] = Σ_{j≤i} M[i,j] · X[j,:]`  → `[Q, P]`.\n87\t\n88\t**(2) Per-chunk state (right factor)**\n89\t- `decay_state[t] = exp(a_last - a[t])`.\n90\t- `states[d, s] = Σ_t decay_state[t] · X[t,d] · B[t,s]`  → `[P, N]` per `(b,chunk,head)`.\n91\t\n92\t**(3) Inter-chunk recurrence (middle factor)**\n93\t- `A_chunk_end[c] = a_last(c)`, padded with a leading 0 → length NC+1.\n94\t- `decay_chunk[i,j] = exp(segsum(A_chunk_end_padded))[i,j]`  → `[NC+1, NC+1]` per `(b,head)`.\n95\t- `new_states[i] = Σ_j decay_chunk[i,j] · states_with_init[j]`, where index 0 is\n96\t  `initial_states`. `states_out = new_states[:-1]`, `final_state = new_states[-1]`.\n97\t  With NC ≤ 4 this is tiny — effectively a short prefix scan over chunks.\n98\t\n99\t**(4) Off-diagonal / state→output (left factor)**\n100\t- `state_decay_out[t] = exp(a[t])`.\n101\t- `Y_off[t,:] = state_decay_out[t] · (C_t · states_out) = exp(a[t]) · Σ_s C[t,s] states_out[s,:]`\n102\t  → `[Q, P]`.\n103\t\n104\t**(5) Combine**\n105\t- `y = Y_diag + Y_off`, reshape to `[B, L_pad, H, P]`, add `D_residual = D[h]·X`,\n106\t  crop to `L`, reshape to `[B, L, H·P]`, cast bf16.\n107\t- `final_state` cast bf16.\n108\t\n109\t### Exploitable properties (optimization levers)\n110\t1. **Head-shared B, C, G** (n_groups=1): compute `G = C Bᵀ`, and the `C·states` /\n111\t   `X·B` contractions, only once per group where possible. G is genuinely head-independent.\n112\t2. **No `[Q,Q]` materialization**: fuse `L`, `M`, and `Y_diag = MX` inside one kernel\n113\t   using `tl.dot` on `[Q,Q]·[Q,P]` with the mask/decay applied in registers/SRAM.\n114\t3. **Tiny inter-chunk stage**: NC ≤ 4 ⇒ the recurrence is cheap; do it with a small\n115\t   kernel (or even a sequential per-chunk scan) rather than a general bmm.\n116\t4. **N=Q=256, P=64**: convenient tile shapes; `[256,256]`, `[256,64]` fit A800 SRAM\n117\t   with fp32 accumulation.\n118\t\n119\t---\n120\t\n121\t## 3. Constraints\n122\t\n123\t- **Isolation**: work only in this workspace; only `KernelWiki` / `ncu-report-skill`\n124\t  skills as external knowledge. No web, subagents, MCP, other agents.\n125\t- **Triton-only compute**; PyTorch allowed only for shapes/allocation/launch. A failed\n126\t  Triton kernel is invalid — must NOT be replaced by a Torch/CPU/NumPy path.\n127\t- **Immutable candidates**: `solution/solution.py` versions `c001`, `c002`, … each with\n128\t  a unique ID + source hash; never reuse an ID for changed source; append one JSON record\n129\t  per evaluated candidate to `candidates.jsonl` (never rewrite earlier records).\n130\t- **Evaluation**: only via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>`. The five fixed\n131\t  workloads together = one candidate evaluation. Budget 100 evals; token soft 1.0M / hard\n132\t  1.2M. `final` (16 workloads) is operator-only.\n133\t- **Forbidden**: running CUDA/profilers/`nvidia-smi`/the external evaluator/any alternate\n134\t  correctness harness directly; modifying evaluator/dataset/controller/launcher/config.\n135\t- Correctness gate per workload: `max_atol` (0.02–0.029), `max_rtol = 0.05`,\n136\t  `required_match_ratio = 0.98`. So up to 2% of elements may exceed tolerance — some slack\n137\t  for a few blown-up exp positions, but the bulk must match the fp32 reference.\n138\t\n139\t---\n140\t\n141\t## 4. Numerical risks & fidelity plan\n142\t\n143\tThe reference casts bf16→fp32, computes everything in fp32, then casts back. To stay in\n144\ttolerance we must **mirror the fp32 math**, especially around the exponentials.\n145\t\n146\t1. **exp overflow / random-walk `a`.** Inputs are `type: random` bf16; `A` is *not*\n147\t   guaranteed negative here. `a[t] = cumsum` of up to 256 values; if A ~ O(1), partial\n148\t   sums random-walk to ±tens, and `exp(a[i]-a[j])` / `exp(a[t])` can reach `exp(±50)`\n149\t   (within fp32 range) or occasionally overflow to `inf`. Whatever the reference produces\n150\t   (including inf at extreme positions) is ground truth, so we replicate exactly:\n151\t   - Always form the **difference first, then exp**: `exp(a[i]-a[j])`, `exp(a_last-a[t])`,\n152\t     matching `segsum` (never `exp(a[i])/exp(a[j])`, which overflows differently).\n153\t   - Keep `a`, cumsum, and all exp arguments in **fp32**.\n154\t   - The 0.98 match ratio absorbs a small number of extreme/inf positions if they occur.\n155\t2. **tf32 vs ieee in `tl.dot`.** A800 `tl.dot` defaults to tf32 (10-bit mantissa) for\n156\t   fp32 inputs. The `C·B`, `M·X`, `X·B`, `C·states` matmuls feed into exp-amplified paths;\n157\t   tf32 error (~1e-3 rel) could be magnified. Plan: start with **fp32 accumulation**;\n158\t   evaluate tf32 (`input_precision=\"tf32\"`) for the big matmuls first (rtol=0.05 is loose),\n159\t   and fall back to `\"ieee\"` selectively if a workload fails match ratio. Treat precision\n160\t   mode as a tunable across candidates.\n161\t3. **bf16 inputs to `tl.dot`.** Could load X/B/C as bf16 and use tf32/bf16 matmul with\n162\t   fp32 accumulate (fast, and the reference itself starts from bf16 data). Risk: extra\n163\t   rounding vs. the reference's explicit fp32 upcast. Validate empirically; bf16 inputs\n164\t   likely fine for the value matmul (`M·X`) but riskier where results are exponentiated.\n165\t4. **cumsum correctness.** Need an in-chunk prefix sum over Q=256 (`tl.cumsum` along the\n166\t   sequence axis) matching `torch.cumsum`. Padding rows contribute A=0 so cumsum stays\n167\t   flat — must load A with `other=0.0` beyond valid length.\n168\t5. **Padding & masking.** Last chunk of L=293/541 has 37/29 valid rows. All seq-indexed\n169\t   loads (X, A, B, C) must mask invalid rows to 0. `state_decay_out`/`decay_state` on\n170\t   padded rows are harmless (multiplied by zeroed data) and those rows are cropped anyway.\n171\t   Output store must be masked to `< L` on the seq axis.\n172\t6. **Accumulation order in states/inter-chunk.** The chunk recurrence sums a decayed\n173\t   `initial_states` (bf16→fp32) with per-chunk states. Keep fp32 throughout; cast to bf16\n174\t   only when writing `final_state` and `output`.\n175\t7. **D residual** added in fp32 before final bf16 cast (`y + D[h]·X`), matching reference.\n176\t8. **Layout / transpose hazards.** `A` is `[B,H,L]` (head-major) while X/B/C are seq-major\n177\t   `[B,L,·]`; strides must be handled explicitly to avoid mis-indexing when chunking.\n178\t\n179\tFidelity acceptance heuristic: aim comfortably inside atol/rtol on the bulk; use match\n180\tratio only as a safety valve for rare exp extremes, not as a crutch for systematic error.\n181\t\n182\t---\n183\t\n184\t## 5. Why the reference is slow (headroom)\n185\t\n186\tThe reference materializes, in fp32 global memory, `L: [B,H,NC,Q,Q]` and\n187\t`G,M: [B,NC,Q,Q,H]`. For (B=4, L=1024): `4·4·256·256·16·4B ≈ 2.1 GB` **each**, plus the\n188\t`einsum('bcihs,bcjhs->bcijh')` over expanded (broadcast) B/C. This is dominated by DRAM\n189\ttraffic on multi-GB intermediates and redundant per-head recomputation of the\n190\thead-independent `G`. A fused Triton kernel that (a) never writes `[Q,Q]` matrices to\n191\tDRAM, (b) computes `G`/CB once per group, and (c) keeps decays in registers should give a\n192\tlarge speedup — the memory-bound reference is the main source of headroom, not raw FLOPs.\n193\t\n194\t---\n195\t\n196\t## 6. Triton design space\n197\t\n198\t### 6a. Kernel decomposition options\n199\t- **Option A — Faithful 4-stage SSD (mamba_ssm-style):**\n200\t  1. `chunk_cumsum`: compute `a = cumsum(A)` per `(b,head,chunk)` (+ `a_last`).\n201\t  2. `chunk_state`: `states[p,n] = Σ_t exp(a_last-a[t])·X[t,p]·B[t,n]` → `[B,NC,H,P,N]`.\n202\t  3. `state_passing / inter-chunk`: apply `decay_chunk` recurrence over NC (+ initial),\n203\t     produce `states_out` and `final_state`.\n204\t  4. `chunk_scan`: fuse `Y_diag = (G∘L)·X` and `Y_off = exp(a)·(C·states_out)`, add D,\n205\t     write output. This mirrors well-known, well-tuned kernels; good default.\n206\t- **Option B — Two big fused kernels:** a \"state\" kernel (stages 1–2) and a \"scan\" kernel\n207\t  (stages 1,3,4 fused with recomputed cumsum). Fewer launches / less intermediate DRAM,\n208\t  more register pressure.\n209\t- **Option C — Aggressive single mega-kernel per `(b,head)`** looping chunks internally to\n210\t  carry the state in registers/SRAM (exploits NC ≤ 4). Removes the inter-chunk kernel and\n211\t  the `states` round-trip entirely, but serializes chunks within a block and raises\n212\t  register/SRAM pressure; attractive because NC is tiny.\n213\t\n214\tPlan: **start with Option A** (clear, matches a proven structure, easy to validate stage\n215\tby stage via the harness), then explore B/C for launch-overhead and DRAM reduction. The\n216\tfirst landed candidate `c001` should prioritize *correctness within tolerance* over peak\n217\tspeed.\n218\t\n219\t### 6b. Tiling & mapping\n220\t- Diagonal (`chunk_scan`) block: program per `(b, chunk, head)`; tile the `i` (query) axis\n221\t  in blocks of e.g. 64/128 rows over Q=256, inner-loop `j` over `≤ i` key blocks; do\n222\t  `tl.dot([BLK_i, Q_kv], [Q_kv, P])` with the lower-tri mask + `exp(a_i-a_j)` decay applied\n223\t  to the CB score tile in SRAM. P=64 fits a single dot N-dim.\n224\t- `chunk_state`: tile over `(p, n)` = `(P=64, N=256)`, reduce over t (Q=256) with decayed\n225\t  X and B. `[P,N]` fp32 accumulator per `(b,chunk,head)` fits SRAM.\n226\t- Reuse head-independent `G`/CB and `C·states` across the 16 heads where the decomposition\n227\t  allows (esp. CB in the diagonal stage).\n228\t- Expose enough grid parallelism for the 64-tile workloads (split head_dim / query blocks\n229\t  to fill 108 SMs).\n230\t\n231\t### 6c. Numeric/dtype knobs (candidate axes)\n232\t- matmul precision: `ieee` vs `tf32`; input dtype bf16 vs fp32 per matmul.\n233\t- `BLOCK` sizes for query/key/state tiling; `num_warps`, `num_stages`.\n234\t- fuse vs. split stages; recompute cumsum vs. reload.\n235\t\n236\t### 6d. Inter-chunk stage\n237\tNC ≤ 4 ⇒ implement as a tiny per-`(b,head)` kernel that (a) builds `decay_chunk` from\n238\t`a_last` values, (b) does the length-(NC+1) recurrence starting from `initial_states`.\n239\tCheap; correctness-critical for `final_state`. Could also be a sequential in-kernel scan.\n240\t\n241\t---\n242\t\n243\t## 7. Validation strategy\n244\t\n245\t- **Harness-only correctness.** Per project rules I will not run CUDA/profilers/an\n246\t  alternate correctness harness locally; the only correctness signal is\n247\t  `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>` over the five fixed workloads (atol/rtol/\n248\t  match-ratio per workload). Each candidate = one evaluation over all five.\n249\t- **Coverage across the five workloads** already exercises the important regimes:\n250\t  - partial last chunk with heavy padding (L=293 → 37 valid; L=541 → 29 valid),\n251\t  - exact multiples (L=256 single chunk; L=1024 four chunks),\n252\t  - batch scaling (B=1,2,4) and thus occupancy variety (64–256 tiles).\n253\t  So passing all five is a strong signal for both correctness (padding/masking, cumsum,\n254\t  decay) and speed generality. Both outputs (`output` and `final_state`) are checked.\n255\t- **Incremental, reasoned candidates.** Build up: `c001` = correct faithful Option-A\n256\t  kernel with conservative precision (fp32/ieee where risky) to lock correctness and get a\n257\t  baseline geomean; subsequent candidates tune precision (tf32/bf16 inputs), tiling,\n258\t  fusion, and grid parallelism, one immutable change per ID.\n259\t- **Regression discipline.** Record per candidate: parent, source hash, hypothesis,\n260\t  per-workload pass/fail + speedup, geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage.\n261\t  Never rewrite earlier records. Keep any candidate that regresses correctness reverted.\n262\t- **Static self-checks before each eval** (no GPU): re-derive shapes/strides, mask\n263\t  boundaries, cumsum axis, exp-difference ordering, and dtype casts against §2/§4 to avoid\n264\t  wasting evaluations on avoidable bugs.\n265\t- **Skills.** Consult `KernelWiki` for A800/Hopper SSD/chunk-scan kernel patterns and\n266\t  `ncu-report-skill` conceptually for bottleneck reasoning (without running the profiler\n267\t  here), and log their usage.\n268\t\n269\t---\n270\t\n271\t## 8. Open questions / risks to resolve during implementation\n272\t\n273\t1. Distribution/scale of `random` inputs — governs how often `exp` blows up and whether\n274\t   tf32 is safe. Resolve empirically via the first evaluations.\n275\t2. Best precision setting per matmul (tf32 vs ieee vs bf16-input) to maximize speed while\n276\t   holding the 0.98 match ratio.\n277\t3. Whether to reuse head-independent `G`/CB explicitly (needs a group-major loop) vs.\n278\t   recompute per head for simplicity in `c001`.\n279\t4. Option A vs. C for the inter-chunk state given NC ≤ 4 (register-carry mega-kernel could\n280\t   remove a whole DRAM round-trip).\n281\t5. Grid/occupancy tuning for the 64-tile workloads on 108 SMs.\n282\t6. Exact stride handling for `A: [B,H,L]` vs seq-major X/B/C during chunking.\n283\t\n284\t---\n285\t\n286\t## 9. Next step\n287\t\n288\tProceed to `docs/plan.md` with an executable, staged plan: `c001` = correct Option-A\n289\tTriton implementation (conservative precision) to establish a passing baseline geomean,\n290\tfollowed by tuned candidates (precision, head-shared reuse, fusion, tiling, occupancy).\n291\tNo solution code before the plan is written.\n292\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d32770ec-e306-49b1-bbd7-91ee447bd6f4","uuid":"db7bda36-e65c-407c-b592-e51aeb530637","timestamp":"2026-09-16T18:03:13.484Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-043_mamba_chunk_scan_with_segsum/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L2/043 Mamba-2 Chunk Scan with Segment Sum\n\nTask: optimize the official SOL-ExecBench task `L2/043_mamba_chunk_scan_with_segsum`\non NVIDIA **A800 (sm_80)**. Primary implementation must be **Triton**; PyTorch only\nfor metadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\nRanking: geometric-mean speedup vs. the reference, subject to every selected workload\npassing correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operation summary\n\nThis is the standard **Mamba-2 SSD (State-Space Duality) chunked parallel scan**. The\nsequence is split into chunks of `chunk_size = 256`; within each chunk an\nattention-like \"diagonal\" block is computed, and across chunks a state recurrence with\nexponential decay is run. Output = intra-chunk (diagonal) + inter-chunk (off-diagonal)\n+ `D` skip connection.\n\n### Fixed dimensions (from `task/definition.json`)\n| symbol | name | value |\n|--------|------|-------|\n| H | num_heads | 16 (const) |\n| P | head_dim | 64 (const) |\n| N | state_size | 256 (const) |\n| G_grp | n_groups | 1 (const) |\n| Q | chunk_size | 256 (const) |\n| B | batch_size | var |\n| L | seq_len | var |\n| — | hidden_out | H·P = 1024 |\n\nNote `N == Q == 256`, `P == 64`. Everything is bf16 on input/output; the reference does\nall math in **float32** and casts back to bf16 at the very end.\n\n### Inputs / outputs\n- `hidden_states` (X): `[B, L, H, P]` bf16 — the \"values\".\n- `A`: `[B, H, L]` bf16 — per-head log-decay (note the H,L order, transposed vs X).\n- `B` param: `[B, L, G_grp=1, N]` bf16 — shared across heads.\n- `C` param: `[B, L, G_grp=1, N]` bf16 — shared across heads.\n- `D`: `[H]` bf16 — skip/residual scale.\n- `initial_states`: `[B, H, P, N]` bf16 — carry-in SSM state.\n- Output `output`: `[B, L, H·P]` bf16.\n- Output `final_state`: `[B, H, P, N]` bf16.\n\n### Padding / chunking\n`pad = (Q - L % Q) % Q`; seq is zero-padded to `L_pad = L + pad`, `NC = L_pad / Q`\nchunks. Padding value 0 flows through cleanly: A-pad=0 keeps cumsum flat, X-pad=0/B-pad=0\ncontribute nothing, and the tail is cropped back to `L` at the end. So partial chunks need\nonly **row masking on loads (other=0)**, no special-case math.\n\n### Per-workload metadata (derived)\n| B | L | pad | L_pad | NC | last-chunk valid rows | tiles (B·NC·H) |\n|---|-----|-----|-------|----|----------------------|----------------|\n| 2 | 293 | 219 | 512 | 2 | 37 | 64 |\n| 4 | 1024| 0   | 1024 | 4 | 256 | 256 |\n| 4 | 256 | 0   | 256  | 1 | 256 | 64 |\n| 1 | 1024| 0   | 1024 | 4 | 256 | 64 |\n| 4 | 541 | 227 | 768  | 3 | 29 | 192 |\n\nParallel work (independent `(b, chunk, head)` tiles) ranges 64–256. A800 has 108 SMs, so\nfor the 64-tile cases occupancy is a first-order concern — the kernel decomposition should\nexpose extra parallelism (e.g. split over head_dim / state, or map heads×batch×chunk to a\nlarge grid) rather than one heavy block per tile.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Algebraic structure (what we actually must compute)\n\nLet, per `(b, chunk, head)`:\n- `a[t] = cumsum_t A[t]` over the chunk (t = 0..Q-1), fp32. `a_last = a[Q-1]`.\n\nThe reference's `segment_sum` is exactly the pairwise cumulative difference:\n`segsum(A)[i,j] = Σ_{k=j+1..i} A[k] = a[i] - a[j]` for `i ≥ j`, else `-inf`.\nTherefore the intra-chunk decay matrix is\n```\nL[i,j] = exp(a[i] - a[j])   for i ≥ j,   0 otherwise   (lower-triangular)\n```\nThis is the crucial simplification: the reference materializes `L` as\n`[B,H,NC,Q,Q]` and `G,M` as `[B,NC,Q,Q,H]` (multi-GB tensors — see §5), but we never\nneed to materialize the `[Q,Q]` (let alone `[Q,Q,H]`) matrices to global memory.\n\n**(1) Intra-chunk / diagonal output**\n- `G[i,j] = C_i · B_j = Σ_s C[i,s] B[j,s]` — **head-independent** because n_groups=1\n  (B, C are broadcast from the single group across all 16 heads). So `G` is computed\n  **once per `(b, chunk)`** and reused by all H heads → up to 16× less CB^T matmul work.\n- `M[i,j] = G[i,j] · L[i,j]` (lower-tri; head-dependent via L).\n- `Y_diag[i,:] = Σ_{j≤i} M[i,j] · X[j,:]`  → `[Q, P]`.\n\n**(2) Per-chunk state (right factor)**\n- `decay_state[t] = exp(a_last - a[t])`.\n- `states[d, s] = Σ_t decay_state[t] · X[t,d] · B[t,s]`  → `[P, N]` per `(b,chunk,head)`.\n\n**(3) Inter-chunk recurrence (middle factor)**\n- `A_chunk_end[c] = a_last(c)`, padded with a leading 0 → length NC+1.\n- `decay_chunk[i,j] = exp(segsum(A_chunk_end_padded))[i,j]`  → `[NC+1, NC+1]` per `(b,head)`.\n- `new_states[i] = Σ_j decay_chunk[i,j] · states_with_init[j]`, where index 0 is\n  `initial_states`. `states_out = new_states[:-1]`, `final_state = new_states[-1]`.\n  With NC ≤ 4 this is tiny — effectively a short prefix scan over chunks.\n\n**(4) Off-diagonal / state→output (left factor)**\n- `state_decay_out[t] = exp(a[t])`.\n- `Y_off[t,:] = state_decay_out[t] · (C_t · states_out) = exp(a[t]) · Σ_s C[t,s] states_out[s,:]`\n  → `[Q, P]`.\n\n**(5) Combine**\n- `y = Y_diag + Y_off`, reshape to `[B, L_pad, H, P]`, add `D_residual = D[h]·X`,\n  crop to `L`, reshape to `[B, L, H·P]`, cast bf16.\n- `final_state` cast bf16.\n\n### Exploitable properties (optimization levers)\n1. **Head-shared B, C, G** (n_groups=1): compute `G = C Bᵀ`, and the `C·states` /\n   `X·B` contractions, only once per group where possible. G is genuinely head-independent.\n2. **No `[Q,Q]` materialization**: fuse `L`, `M`, and `Y_diag = MX` inside one kernel\n   using `tl.dot` on `[Q,Q]·[Q,P]` with the mask/decay applied in registers/SRAM.\n3. **Tiny inter-chunk stage**: NC ≤ 4 ⇒ the recurrence is cheap; do it with a small\n   kernel (or even a sequential per-chunk scan) rather than a general bmm.\n4. **N=Q=256, P=64**: convenient tile shapes; `[256,256]`, `[256,64]` fit A800 SRAM\n   with fp32 accumulation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Constraints\n\n- **Isolation**: work only in this workspace; only `KernelWiki` / `ncu-report-skill`\n  skills as external knowledge. No web, subagents, MCP, other agents.\n- **Triton-only compute**; PyTorch allowed only for shapes/allocation/launch. A failed\n  Triton kernel is invalid — must NOT be replaced by a Torch/CPU/NumPy path.\n- **Immutable candidates**: `solution/solution.py` versions `c001`, `c002`, … each with\n  a unique ID + source hash; never reuse an ID for changed source; append one JSON record\n  per evaluated candidate to `candidates.jsonl` (never rewrite earlier records).\n- **Evaluation**: only via `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>`. The five fixed\n  workloads together = one candidate evaluation. Budget 100 evals; token soft 1.0M / hard\n  1.2M. `final` (16 workloads) is operator-only.\n- **Forbidden**: running CUDA/profilers/`nvidia-smi`/the external evaluator/any alternate\n  correctness harness directly; modifying evaluator/dataset/controller/launcher/config.\n- Correctness gate per workload: `max_atol` (0.02–0.029), `max_rtol = 0.05`,\n  `required_match_ratio = 0.98`. So up to 2% of elements may exceed tolerance — some slack\n  for a few blown-up exp positions, but the bulk must match the fp32 reference.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Numerical risks & fidelity plan\n\nThe reference casts bf16→fp32, computes everything in fp32, then casts back. To stay in\ntolerance we must **mirror the fp32 math**, especially around the exponentials.\n\n1. **exp overflow / random-walk `a`.** Inputs are `type: random` bf16; `A` is *not*\n   guaranteed negative here. `a[t] = cumsum` of up to 256 values; if A ~ O(1), partial\n   sums random-walk to ±tens, and `exp(a[i]-a[j])` / `exp(a[t])` can reach `exp(±50)`\n   (within fp32 range) or occasionally overflow to `inf`. Whatever the reference produces\n   (including inf at extreme positions) is ground truth, so we replicate exactly:\n   - Always form the **difference first, then exp**: `exp(a[i]-a[j])`, `exp(a_last-a[t])`,\n     matching `segsum` (never `exp(a[i])/exp(a[j])`, which overflows differently).\n   - Keep `a`, cumsum, and all exp arguments in **fp32**.\n   - The 0.98 match ratio absorbs a small number of extreme/inf positions if they occur.\n2. **tf32 vs ieee in `tl.dot`.** A800 `tl.dot` defaults to tf32 (10-bit mantissa) for\n   fp32 inputs. The `C·B`, `M·X`, `X·B`, `C·states` matmuls feed into exp-amplified paths;\n   tf32 error (~1e-3 rel) could be magnified. Plan: start with **fp32 accumulation**;\n   evaluate tf32 (`input_precision=\"tf32\"`) for the big matmuls first (rtol=0.05 is loose),\n   and fall back to `\"ieee\"` selectively if a workload fails match ratio. Treat precision\n   mode as a tunable across candidates.\n3. **bf16 inputs to `tl.dot`.** Could load X/B/C as bf16 and use tf32/bf16 matmul with\n   fp32 accumulate (fast, and the reference itself starts from bf16 data). Risk: extra\n   rounding vs. the reference's explicit fp32 upcast. Validate empirically; bf16 inputs\n   likely fine for the value matmul (`M·X`) but riskier where results are exponentiated.\n4. **cumsum correctness.** Need an in-chunk prefix sum over Q=256 (`tl.cumsum` along the\n   sequence axis) matching `torch.cumsum`. Padding rows contribute A=0 so cumsum stays\n   flat — must load A with `other=0.0` beyond valid length.\n5. **Padding & masking.** Last chunk of L=293/541 has 37/29 valid rows. All seq-indexed\n   loads (X, A, B, C) must mask invalid rows to 0. `state_decay_out`/`decay_state` on\n   padded rows are harmless (multiplied by zeroed data) and those rows are cropped anyway.\n   Output store must be masked to `< L` on the seq axis.\n6. **Accumulation order in states/inter-chunk.** The chunk recurrence sums a decayed\n   `initial_states` (bf16→fp32) with per-chunk states. Keep fp32 throughout; cast to bf16\n   only when writing `final_state` and `output`.\n7. **D residual** added in fp32 before final bf16 cast (`y + D[h]·X`), matching reference.\n8. **Layout / transpose hazards.** `A` is `[B,H,L]` (head-major) while X/B/C are seq-major\n   `[B,L,·]`; strides must be handled explicitly to avoid mis-indexing when chunking.\n\nFidelity acceptance heuristic: aim comfortably inside atol/rtol on the bulk; use match\nratio only as a safety valve for rare exp extremes, not as a crutch for systematic error.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Why the reference is slow (headroom)\n\nThe reference materializes, in fp32 global memory, `L: [B,H,NC,Q,Q]` and\n`G,M: [B,NC,Q,Q,H]`. For (B=4, L=1024): `4·4·256·256·16·4B ≈ 2.1 GB` **each**, plus the\n`einsum('bcihs,bcjhs->bcijh')` over expanded (broadcast) B/C. This is dominated by DRAM\ntraffic on multi-GB intermediates and redundant per-head recomputation of the\nhead-independent `G`. A fused Triton kernel that (a) never writes `[Q,Q]` matrices to\nDRAM, (b) computes `G`/CB once per group, and (c) keeps decays in registers should give a\nlarge speedup — the memory-bound reference is the main source of headroom, not raw FLOPs.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Triton design space\n\n### 6a. Kernel decomposition options\n- **Option A — Faithful 4-stage SSD (mamba_ssm-style):**\n  1. `chunk_cumsum`: compute `a = cumsum(A)` per `(b,head,chunk)` (+ `a_last`).\n  2. `chunk_state`: `states[p,n] = Σ_t exp(a_last-a[t])·X[t,p]·B[t,n]` → `[B,NC,H,P,N]`.\n  3. `state_passing / inter-chunk`: apply `decay_chunk` recurrence over NC (+ initial),\n     produce `states_out` and `final_state`.\n  4. `chunk_scan`: fuse `Y_diag = (G∘L)·X` and `Y_off = exp(a)·(C·states_out)`, add D,\n     write output. This mirrors well-known, well-tuned kernels; good default.\n- **Option B — Two big fused kernels:** a \"state\" kernel (stages 1–2) and a \"scan\" kernel\n  (stages 1,3,4 fused with recomputed cumsum). Fewer launches / less intermediate DRAM,\n  more register pressure.\n- **Option C — Aggressive single mega-kernel per `(b,head)`** looping chunks internally to\n  carry the state in registers/SRAM (exploits NC ≤ 4). Removes the inter-chunk kernel and\n  the `states` round-trip entirely, but serializes chunks within a block and raises\n  register/SRAM pressure; attractive because NC is tiny.\n\nPlan: **start with Option A** (clear, matches a proven structure, easy to validate stage\nby stage via the harness), then explore B/C for launch-overhead and DRAM reduction. The\nfirst landed candidate `c001` should prioritize *correctness within tolerance* over peak\nspeed.\n\n### 6b. Tiling & mapping\n- Diagonal (`chunk_scan`) block: program per `(b, chunk, head)`; tile the `i` (query) axis\n  in blocks of e.g. 64/128 rows over Q=256, inner-loop `j` over `≤ i` key blocks; do\n  `tl.dot([BLK_i, Q_kv], [Q_kv, P])` with the lower-tri mask + `exp(a_i-a_j)` decay applied\n  to the CB score tile in SRAM. P=64 fits a single dot N-dim.\n- `chunk_state`: tile over `(p, n)` = `(P=64, N=256)`, reduce over t (Q=256) with decayed\n  X and B. `[P,N]` fp32 accumulator per `(b,chunk,head)` fits SRAM.\n- Reuse head-independent `G`/CB and `C·states` across the 16 heads where the decomposition\n  allows (esp. CB in the diagonal stage).\n- Expose enough grid parallelism for the 64-tile workloads (split head_dim / query blocks\n  to fill 108 SMs).\n\n### 6c. Numeric/dtype knobs (candidate axes)\n- matmul precision: `ieee` vs `tf32`; input dtype bf16 vs fp32 per matmul.\n- `BLOCK` sizes for query/key/state tiling; `num_warps`, `num_stages`.\n- fuse vs. split stages; recompute cumsum vs. reload.\n\n### 6d. Inter-chunk stage\nNC ≤ 4 ⇒ implement as a tiny per-`(b,head)` kernel that (a) builds `decay_chunk` from\n`a_last` values, (b) does the length-(NC+1) recurrence starting from `initial_states`.\nCheap; correctness-critical for `final_state`. Could also be a sequential in-kernel scan.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Validation strategy\n\n- **Harness-only correctness.** Per project rules I will not run CUDA/profilers/an\n  alternate correctness harness locally; the only correctness signal is\n  `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback <id>` over the five fixed workloads (atol/rtol/\n  match-ratio per workload). Each candidate = one evaluation over all five.\n- **Coverage across the five workloads** already exercises the important regimes:\n  - partial last chunk with heavy padding (L=293 → 37 valid; L=541 → 29 valid),\n  - exact multiples (L=256 single chunk; L=1024 four chunks),\n  - batch scaling (B=1,2,4) and thus occupancy variety (64–256 tiles).\n  So passing all five is a strong signal for both correctness (padding/masking, cumsum,\n  decay) and speed generality. Both outputs (`output` and `final_state`) are checked.\n- **Incremental, reasoned candidates.** Build up: `c001` = correct faithful Option-A\n  kernel with conservative precision (fp32/ieee where risky) to lock correctness and get a\n  baseline geomean; subsequent candidates tune precision (tf32/bf16 inputs), tiling,\n  fusion, and grid parallelism, one immutable change per ID.\n- **Regression discipline.** Record per candidate: parent, source hash, hypothesis,\n  per-workload pass/fail + speedup, geomean, decision, cumulative eval count, skill usage.\n  Never rewrite earlier records. Keep any candidate that regresses correctness reverted.\n- **Static self-checks before each eval** (no GPU): re-derive shapes/strides, mask\n  boundaries, cumsum axis, exp-difference ordering, and dtype casts against §2/§4 to avoid\n  wasting evaluations on avoidable bugs.\n- **Skills.** Consult `KernelWiki` for A800/Hopper SSD/chunk-scan kernel patterns and\n  `ncu-report-skill` conceptually for bottleneck reasoning (without running the profiler\n  here), and log their usage.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Open questions / risks to resolve during implementation\n\n1. Distribution/scale of `random` inputs — governs how often `exp` blows up and whether\n   tf32 is safe. Resolve empirically via the first evaluations.\n2. Best precision setting per matmul (tf32 vs ieee vs bf16-input) to maximize speed while\n   holding the 0.98 match ratio.\n3. Whether to reuse head-independent `G`/CB explicitly (needs a group-major loop) vs.\n   recompute per head for simplicity in `c001`.\n4. Option A vs. C for the inter-chunk state given NC ≤ 4 (register-carry mega-kernel could\n   remove a whole DRAM round-trip).\n5. Grid/occupancy tuning for the 64-tile workloads on 108 SMs.\n6. Exact stride handling for `A: [B,H,L]` vs seq-major X/B/C during chunking.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Next step\n\nProceed to `docs/plan.md` with an executable, staged plan: `c001` = correct Option-A\nTriton implementation (conservative precision) to establish a passing baseline geomean,\nfollowed by tuned candidates (precision, head-shared reuse, fusion, tiling, occupancy).\nNo solution code before the plan is written.\n","numLines":292,"startLine":1,"totalLines":292}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FUjYW3gUhLaNAPVHzfjEBj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t    \"name\": \"043_mamba_chunk_scan_with_segsum\",\n3\t    \"hf_id\": \"ibm-granite/granite-4.0-1b\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Mamba-2 chunk-based parallel scan with segment sum computation for intra-chunk and inter-chunk state propagation. This is the core parallel scanning algorithm that enables efficient SSM computation by breaking sequences into chunks (chunk_size=256), computing intra-chunk attention-like patterns using segment sums, maintaining inter-chunk state recurrence with exponential decay, and combining diagonal (intra-chunk) and off-diagonal (inter-chunk) contributions.\",\n5\t    \"axes\": {\n6\t        \"batch_size\": {\n7\t            \"type\": \"var\"\n8\t        },\n9\t        \"seq_len\": {\n10\t            \"type\": \"var\"\n11\t        },\n12\t        \"num_heads\": {\n13\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n14\t            \"value\": 16\n15\t        },\n16\t        \"head_dim\": {\n17\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n18\t            \"value\": 64\n19\t        },\n20\t        \"state_size\": {\n21\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n22\t            \"value\": 256\n23\t        },\n24\t        \"n_groups\": {\n25\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n26\t            \"value\": 1\n27\t        },\n28\t        \"chunk_size\": {\n29\t            \"type\": \"const\",\n30\t            \"value\": 256\n31\t        },\n32\t        \"hidden_out\": {\n33\t            \"type\": \"expr\",\n34\t            \"expression\": \"num_heads * head_dim\"\n35\t        }\n36\t    },\n37\t    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": null,\n38\t    \"inputs\": {\n39\t        \"hidden_states\": {\n40\t            \"shape\": [\n41\t                \"batch_size\",\n42\t                \"seq_len\",\n43\t                \"num_heads\",\n44\t                \"head_dim\"\n45\t            ],\n46\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n47\t            \"description\": \"Input hidden states after discretization\"\n48\t        },\n49\t        \"A\": {\n50\t            \"shape\": [\n51\t                \"batch_size\",\n52\t                \"num_heads\",\n53\t                \"seq_len\"\n54\t            ],\n55\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n56\t            \"description\": \"Discretized A parameter (negative log decay)\"\n57\t        },\n58\t        \"B\": {\n59\t            \"shape\": [\n60\t                \"batch_size\",\n61\t                \"seq_len\",\n62\t                \"n_groups\",\n63\t                \"state_size\"\n64\t            ],\n65\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n66\t            \"description\": \"Discretized B parameter\"\n67\t        },\n68\t        \"C\": {\n69\t            \"shape\": [\n70\t                \"batch_size\",\n71\t                \"seq_len\",\n72\t                \"n_groups\",\n73\t                \"state_size\"\n74\t            ],\n75\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n76\t            \"description\": \"C parameter for state-to-output projection\"\n77\t        },\n78\t        \"D\": {\n79\t            \"shape\": [\n80\t                \"num_heads\"\n81\t            ],\n82\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n83\t            \"description\": \"Skip connection parameter\"\n84\t        },\n85\t        \"initial_states\": {\n86\t            \"shape\": [\n87\t                \"batch_size\",\n88\t                \"num_heads\",\n89\t                \"head_dim\",\n90\t                \"state_size\"\n91\t            ],\n92\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n93\t            \"description\": \"Initial SSM state\"\n94\t        }\n95\t    },\n96\t    \"outputs\": {\n97\t        \"output\": {\n98\t            \"shape\": [\n99\t                \"batch_size\",\n100\t                \"seq_len\",\n101\t                \"hidden_out\"\n102\t            ],\n103\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n104\t            \"description\": \"Scan output\"\n105\t        },\n106\t        \"final_state\": {\n107\t            \"shape\": [\n108\t                \"batch_size\",\n109\t                \"num_heads\",\n110\t                \"head_dim\",\n111\t                \"state_size\"\n112\t            ],\n113\t            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n114\t            \"description\": \"Final SSM state for next chunk\"\n115\t        }\n116\t    },\n117\t    \"reference\": \"import torch\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\n\\n\\ndef segment_sum(input_tensor: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Compute segment sum (cumulative sum with lower triangular masking).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    chunk_size = input_tensor.size(-1)\\n    input_tensor = input_tensor[..., None].expand(*input_tensor.size(), chunk_size)\\n    mask = torch.tril(\\n        torch.ones(chunk_size, chunk_size, device=input_tensor.device, dtype=torch.bool),\\n        diagonal=-1\\n    )\\n    input_tensor = input_tensor.masked_fill(~mask, 0)\\n    tensor_segsum = torch.cumsum(input_tensor, dim=-2)\\n    mask = torch.tril(\\n        torch.ones(chunk_size, chunk_size, device=input_tensor.device, dtype=torch.bool),\\n        diagonal=0\\n    )\\n    tensor_segsum = tensor_segsum.masked_fill(~mask, float('-inf'))\\n    return tensor_segsum\\n\\n\\ndef pad_tensor_by_size(input_tensor: torch.Tensor, pad_size: int) -> torch.Tensor:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Pad tensor on seq_len dimension.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if pad_size == 0:\\n        return input_tensor\\n    if len(input_tensor.shape) == 4:\\n        pad_shape = (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, pad_size, 0, 0)\\n    else:\\n        pad_shape = (0, 0, 0, pad_size, 0, 0)\\n    return F.pad(input_tensor, pad_shape, mode='constant', value=0)\\n\\n\\ndef reshape_into_chunks(input_tensor: torch.Tensor, pad_size: int, chunk_size: int) -> torch.Tensor:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Reshape tensor into chunks after padding.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    input_tensor = pad_tensor_by_size(input_tensor, pad_size)\\n    if len(input_tensor.shape) == 3:\\n        return input_tensor.reshape(\\n            input_tensor.shape[0], -1, chunk_size, input_tensor.shape[2]\\n        )\\n    else:\\n        return input_tensor.reshape(\\n            input_tensor.shape[0], -1, chunk_size, input_tensor.shape[2], input_tensor.shape[3]\\n        )\\n\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    A: torch.Tensor,\\n    B: torch.Tensor,\\n    C: torch.Tensor,\\n    D: torch.Tensor,\\n    initial_states: torch.Tensor,\\n):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Mamba-2 chunk-based parallel scan with segment sum.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim = hidden_states.shape\\n    state_size = 256\\n    n_groups = 1\\n    chunk_size = 256\\n    \\n    # Compute padding size to make seq_len multiple of chunk_size\\n    pad_size = (chunk_size - seq_len % chunk_size) % chunk_size\\n    \\n    # Convert to float32 for numerical stability\\n    hidden_states_f = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    A_f = A.to(torch.float32)\\n    B_f = B.to(torch.float32)\\n    C_f = C.to(torch.float32)\\n    D_f = D.to(torch.float32)\\n    initial_states_f = initial_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    \\n    # Expand B and C to match num_heads (from n_groups=1 to num_heads=16)\\n    # [batch, seq_len, 1, state_size] -> [batch, seq_len, 16, state_size]\\n    B_expanded = B_f.expand(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, state_size)\\n    C_expanded = C_f.expand(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, state_size)\\n    \\n    # Apply D residual (before chunking)\\n    hidden_states_padded = pad_tensor_by_size(hidden_states_f, pad_size)\\n    D_residual = D_f[None, None, :, None] * hidden_states_padded  # [batch, seq_len_padded, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    # Reshape into chunks\\n    hidden_states_chunked = reshape_into_chunks(hidden_states_f, pad_size, chunk_size)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    A_transposed = A_f.transpose(1, 2)  # [batch, seq_len, num_heads]\\n    A_chunked = reshape_into_chunks(A_transposed, pad_size, chunk_size)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    B_chunked = reshape_into_chunks(B_expanded, pad_size, chunk_size)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    \\n    C_chunked = reshape_into_chunks(C_expanded, pad_size, chunk_size)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    \\n    num_chunks = A_chunked.shape[1]\\n    \\n    # Permute A for cumsum: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads] -> [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    A_chunked_perm = A_chunked.permute(0, 3, 1, 2)\\n    A_cumsum = torch.cumsum(A_chunked_perm, dim=-1)\\n    # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    \\n    # 1. Compute intra-chunk outputs (diagonal blocks)\\n    # L matrix: exponential of segment sum of A\\n    L = torch.exp(segment_sum(A_chunked_perm))  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size]\\n    \\n    # Compute G: contraction of C and B over state_size\\n    # C_chunked: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # B_chunked: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # G[b, nc, i, j, h] = sum_s C[b, nc, i, h, s] * B[b, nc, j, h, s]\\n    G = torch.einsum('bcihs,bcjhs->bcijh', C_chunked, B_chunked)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # Compute M: apply L (attention-like pattern) to G\\n    # L: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size]\\n    # G: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    # Permute L to [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    L_perm = L.permute(0, 2, 3, 4, 1)  # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    M = G * L_perm  # element-wise, [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # Apply M to hidden_states (like attention to values)\\n    # M: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, chunk_size_j, num_heads]\\n    # hidden_states_chunked: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_j, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    # Y_diag[b, nc, i, h, d] = sum_j M[b, nc, i, j, h] * hidden_states[b, nc, j, h, d]\\n    Y_diag = torch.einsum('bcijh,bcjhd->bcihd', M, hidden_states_chunked)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    # 2. Compute states for each chunk (right term of factorization)\\n    # decay_states: exp(A_cumsum[:, :, :, -1:] - A_cumsum)\\n    # A_cumsum: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    decay_states = torch.exp(A_cumsum[:, :, :, -1:] - A_cumsum)  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    # Permute to [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    decay_states_perm = decay_states.permute(0, 2, 3, 1)  # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # B_decay: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    B_decay = B_chunked * decay_states_perm[..., None]\\n    \\n    # Compute states: sum over chunk_size dimension\\n    # states[b, nc, h, d, s] = sum_t B_decay[b, nc, t, h, s] * hidden_states[b, nc, t, h, d]\\n    states = torch.einsum('bcths,bcthd->bchds', B_decay, hidden_states_chunked)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    \\n    # 3. Compute inter-chunk recurrence (middle term)\\n    # Prepend initial state\\n    # initial_states_f: [batch, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    # states: [batch, num_chunks, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    # Need to transpose states to [batch, num_chunks, num_heads, head_dim, state_size] -> already in this form\\n    # Prepend: [batch, 1, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    initial_states_expanded = initial_states_f[:, None, :, :, :]  # [batch, 1, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    states_with_init = torch.cat([initial_states_expanded, states], dim=1)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks+1, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    \\n    # Compute decay between chunks\\n    # A_cumsum[:, :, :, -1]: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks]\\n    A_chunk_ends = A_cumsum[:, :, :, -1]  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks]\\n    A_chunk_ends_padded = F.pad(A_chunk_ends, (1, 0))  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks+1]\\n    decay_chunk = torch.exp(segment_sum(A_chunk_ends_padded))  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks+1, num_chunks+1]\\n    \\n    # Apply decay to propagate states across chunks\\n    # decay_chunk: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks+1, num_chunks+1]\\n    # states_with_init: [batch, num_chunks+1, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    # new_states[b, i, h, d, s] = sum_j decay_chunk[b, h, i, j] * states_with_init[b, j, h, d, s]\\n    new_states = torch.einsum('bhij,bjhds->bihds', decay_chunk, states_with_init)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks+1, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    \\n    states_out = new_states[:, :-1]  # [batch, num_chunks, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    final_state = new_states[:, -1]  # [batch, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    \\n    # 4. Compute state -> output conversion (left term)\\n    state_decay_out = torch.exp(A_cumsum)  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    state_decay_out_perm = state_decay_out.permute(0, 2, 3, 1)  # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # C_times_states: contract C with states over state_size\\n    # C_chunked: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # states_out: [batch, num_chunks, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    # Y_off[b, nc, t, h, d] = sum_s C[b, nc, t, h, s] * states[b, nc, h, d, s] * decay[b, nc, t, h]\\n    C_times_states = torch.einsum('bcths,bchds->bcthd', C_chunked, states_out)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    Y_off = C_times_states * state_decay_out_perm[..., None]\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    # 5. Combine intra-chunk and inter-chunk outputs\\n    y = Y_diag + Y_off\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    # Reshape back: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim] -> [batch, seq_len_padded, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    y = y.reshape(batch_size, -1, num_heads, head_dim)\\n    \\n    # Add D residual\\n    y = y + D_residual\\n    \\n    # Remove padding\\n    if pad_size > 0:\\n        y = y[:, :seq_len, :, :]\\n    \\n    # Reshape to [batch, seq_len, num_heads * head_dim]\\n    output = y.reshape(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads * head_dim).to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    final_state = final_state.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    return output, final_state\\n\"\n118\t}\n119\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d32770ec-e306-49b1-bbd7-91ee447bd6f4","uuid":"bb7ac100-6a6b-4493-9dc0-85ccc3c555e7","timestamp":"2026-09-16T18:03:13.890Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-043_mamba_chunk_scan_with_segsum/task/definition.json","content":"{\n    \"name\": \"043_mamba_chunk_scan_with_segsum\",\n    \"hf_id\": \"ibm-granite/granite-4.0-1b\",\n    \"description\": \"Mamba-2 chunk-based parallel scan with segment sum computation for intra-chunk and inter-chunk state propagation. This is the core parallel scanning algorithm that enables efficient SSM computation by breaking sequences into chunks (chunk_size=256), computing intra-chunk attention-like patterns using segment sums, maintaining inter-chunk state recurrence with exponential decay, and combining diagonal (intra-chunk) and off-diagonal (inter-chunk) contributions.\",\n    \"axes\": {\n        \"batch_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\"\n        },\n        \"seq_len\": {\n            \"type\": \"var\"\n        },\n        \"num_heads\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 16\n        },\n        \"head_dim\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 64\n        },\n        \"state_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 256\n        },\n        \"n_groups\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 1\n        },\n        \"chunk_size\": {\n            \"type\": \"const\",\n            \"value\": 256\n        },\n        \"hidden_out\": {\n            \"type\": \"expr\",\n            \"expression\": \"num_heads * head_dim\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"custom_inputs_entrypoint\": null,\n    \"inputs\": {\n        \"hidden_states\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"seq_len\",\n                \"num_heads\",\n                \"head_dim\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Input hidden states after discretization\"\n        },\n        \"A\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"num_heads\",\n                \"seq_len\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Discretized A parameter (negative log decay)\"\n        },\n        \"B\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"seq_len\",\n                \"n_groups\",\n                \"state_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Discretized B parameter\"\n        },\n        \"C\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"seq_len\",\n                \"n_groups\",\n                \"state_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"C parameter for state-to-output projection\"\n        },\n        \"D\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"num_heads\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Skip connection parameter\"\n        },\n        \"initial_states\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"num_heads\",\n                \"head_dim\",\n                \"state_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Initial SSM state\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"outputs\": {\n        \"output\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"seq_len\",\n                \"hidden_out\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Scan output\"\n        },\n        \"final_state\": {\n            \"shape\": [\n                \"batch_size\",\n                \"num_heads\",\n                \"head_dim\",\n                \"state_size\"\n            ],\n            \"dtype\": \"bfloat16\",\n            \"description\": \"Final SSM state for next chunk\"\n        }\n    },\n    \"reference\": \"import torch\\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\\n\\n\\ndef segment_sum(input_tensor: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Compute segment sum (cumulative sum with lower triangular masking).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    chunk_size = input_tensor.size(-1)\\n    input_tensor = input_tensor[..., None].expand(*input_tensor.size(), chunk_size)\\n    mask = torch.tril(\\n        torch.ones(chunk_size, chunk_size, device=input_tensor.device, dtype=torch.bool),\\n        diagonal=-1\\n    )\\n    input_tensor = input_tensor.masked_fill(~mask, 0)\\n    tensor_segsum = torch.cumsum(input_tensor, dim=-2)\\n    mask = torch.tril(\\n        torch.ones(chunk_size, chunk_size, device=input_tensor.device, dtype=torch.bool),\\n        diagonal=0\\n    )\\n    tensor_segsum = tensor_segsum.masked_fill(~mask, float('-inf'))\\n    return tensor_segsum\\n\\n\\ndef pad_tensor_by_size(input_tensor: torch.Tensor, pad_size: int) -> torch.Tensor:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Pad tensor on seq_len dimension.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if pad_size == 0:\\n        return input_tensor\\n    if len(input_tensor.shape) == 4:\\n        pad_shape = (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, pad_size, 0, 0)\\n    else:\\n        pad_shape = (0, 0, 0, pad_size, 0, 0)\\n    return F.pad(input_tensor, pad_shape, mode='constant', value=0)\\n\\n\\ndef reshape_into_chunks(input_tensor: torch.Tensor, pad_size: int, chunk_size: int) -> torch.Tensor:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Reshape tensor into chunks after padding.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    input_tensor = pad_tensor_by_size(input_tensor, pad_size)\\n    if len(input_tensor.shape) == 3:\\n        return input_tensor.reshape(\\n            input_tensor.shape[0], -1, chunk_size, input_tensor.shape[2]\\n        )\\n    else:\\n        return input_tensor.reshape(\\n            input_tensor.shape[0], -1, chunk_size, input_tensor.shape[2], input_tensor.shape[3]\\n        )\\n\\n\\n@torch.no_grad()\\ndef run(\\n    hidden_states: torch.Tensor,\\n    A: torch.Tensor,\\n    B: torch.Tensor,\\n    C: torch.Tensor,\\n    D: torch.Tensor,\\n    initial_states: torch.Tensor,\\n):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Mamba-2 chunk-based parallel scan with segment sum.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim = hidden_states.shape\\n    state_size = 256\\n    n_groups = 1\\n    chunk_size = 256\\n    \\n    # Compute padding size to make seq_len multiple of chunk_size\\n    pad_size = (chunk_size - seq_len % chunk_size) % chunk_size\\n    \\n    # Convert to float32 for numerical stability\\n    hidden_states_f = hidden_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    A_f = A.to(torch.float32)\\n    B_f = B.to(torch.float32)\\n    C_f = C.to(torch.float32)\\n    D_f = D.to(torch.float32)\\n    initial_states_f = initial_states.to(torch.float32)\\n    \\n    # Expand B and C to match num_heads (from n_groups=1 to num_heads=16)\\n    # [batch, seq_len, 1, state_size] -> [batch, seq_len, 16, state_size]\\n    B_expanded = B_f.expand(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, state_size)\\n    C_expanded = C_f.expand(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, state_size)\\n    \\n    # Apply D residual (before chunking)\\n    hidden_states_padded = pad_tensor_by_size(hidden_states_f, pad_size)\\n    D_residual = D_f[None, None, :, None] * hidden_states_padded  # [batch, seq_len_padded, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    # Reshape into chunks\\n    hidden_states_chunked = reshape_into_chunks(hidden_states_f, pad_size, chunk_size)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    A_transposed = A_f.transpose(1, 2)  # [batch, seq_len, num_heads]\\n    A_chunked = reshape_into_chunks(A_transposed, pad_size, chunk_size)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    B_chunked = reshape_into_chunks(B_expanded, pad_size, chunk_size)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    \\n    C_chunked = reshape_into_chunks(C_expanded, pad_size, chunk_size)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    \\n    num_chunks = A_chunked.shape[1]\\n    \\n    # Permute A for cumsum: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads] -> [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    A_chunked_perm = A_chunked.permute(0, 3, 1, 2)\\n    A_cumsum = torch.cumsum(A_chunked_perm, dim=-1)\\n    # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    \\n    # 1. Compute intra-chunk outputs (diagonal blocks)\\n    # L matrix: exponential of segment sum of A\\n    L = torch.exp(segment_sum(A_chunked_perm))  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size]\\n    \\n    # Compute G: contraction of C and B over state_size\\n    # C_chunked: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # B_chunked: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # G[b, nc, i, j, h] = sum_s C[b, nc, i, h, s] * B[b, nc, j, h, s]\\n    G = torch.einsum('bcihs,bcjhs->bcijh', C_chunked, B_chunked)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # Compute M: apply L (attention-like pattern) to G\\n    # L: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size]\\n    # G: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    # Permute L to [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    L_perm = L.permute(0, 2, 3, 4, 1)  # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    M = G * L_perm  # element-wise, [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # Apply M to hidden_states (like attention to values)\\n    # M: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_i, chunk_size_j, num_heads]\\n    # hidden_states_chunked: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size_j, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    # Y_diag[b, nc, i, h, d] = sum_j M[b, nc, i, j, h] * hidden_states[b, nc, j, h, d]\\n    Y_diag = torch.einsum('bcijh,bcjhd->bcihd', M, hidden_states_chunked)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    # 2. Compute states for each chunk (right term of factorization)\\n    # decay_states: exp(A_cumsum[:, :, :, -1:] - A_cumsum)\\n    # A_cumsum: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    decay_states = torch.exp(A_cumsum[:, :, :, -1:] - A_cumsum)  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    # Permute to [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    decay_states_perm = decay_states.permute(0, 2, 3, 1)  # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # B_decay: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    B_decay = B_chunked * decay_states_perm[..., None]\\n    \\n    # Compute states: sum over chunk_size dimension\\n    # states[b, nc, h, d, s] = sum_t B_decay[b, nc, t, h, s] * hidden_states[b, nc, t, h, d]\\n    states = torch.einsum('bcths,bcthd->bchds', B_decay, hidden_states_chunked)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    \\n    # 3. Compute inter-chunk recurrence (middle term)\\n    # Prepend initial state\\n    # initial_states_f: [batch, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    # states: [batch, num_chunks, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    # Need to transpose states to [batch, num_chunks, num_heads, head_dim, state_size] -> already in this form\\n    # Prepend: [batch, 1, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    initial_states_expanded = initial_states_f[:, None, :, :, :]  # [batch, 1, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    states_with_init = torch.cat([initial_states_expanded, states], dim=1)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks+1, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    \\n    # Compute decay between chunks\\n    # A_cumsum[:, :, :, -1]: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks]\\n    A_chunk_ends = A_cumsum[:, :, :, -1]  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks]\\n    A_chunk_ends_padded = F.pad(A_chunk_ends, (1, 0))  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks+1]\\n    decay_chunk = torch.exp(segment_sum(A_chunk_ends_padded))  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks+1, num_chunks+1]\\n    \\n    # Apply decay to propagate states across chunks\\n    # decay_chunk: [batch, num_heads, num_chunks+1, num_chunks+1]\\n    # states_with_init: [batch, num_chunks+1, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    # new_states[b, i, h, d, s] = sum_j decay_chunk[b, h, i, j] * states_with_init[b, j, h, d, s]\\n    new_states = torch.einsum('bhij,bjhds->bihds', decay_chunk, states_with_init)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks+1, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    \\n    states_out = new_states[:, :-1]  # [batch, num_chunks, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    final_state = new_states[:, -1]  # [batch, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    \\n    # 4. Compute state -> output conversion (left term)\\n    state_decay_out = torch.exp(A_cumsum)  # [batch, num_heads, num_chunks, chunk_size]\\n    state_decay_out_perm = state_decay_out.permute(0, 2, 3, 1)  # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads]\\n    \\n    # C_times_states: contract C with states over state_size\\n    # C_chunked: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, state_size]\\n    # states_out: [batch, num_chunks, num_heads, head_dim, state_size]\\n    # Y_off[b, nc, t, h, d] = sum_s C[b, nc, t, h, s] * states[b, nc, h, d, s] * decay[b, nc, t, h]\\n    C_times_states = torch.einsum('bcths,bchds->bcthd', C_chunked, states_out)\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    Y_off = C_times_states * state_decay_out_perm[..., None]\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    # 5. Combine intra-chunk and inter-chunk outputs\\n    y = Y_diag + Y_off\\n    # [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    \\n    # Reshape back: [batch, num_chunks, chunk_size, num_heads, head_dim] -> [batch, seq_len_padded, num_heads, head_dim]\\n    y = y.reshape(batch_size, -1, num_heads, head_dim)\\n    \\n    # Add D residual\\n    y = y + D_residual\\n    \\n    # Remove padding\\n    if pad_size > 0:\\n        y = y[:, :seq_len, :, :]\\n    \\n    # Reshape to [batch, seq_len, num_heads * head_dim]\\n    output = y.reshape(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads * head_dim).to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    final_state = final_state.to(torch.bfloat16)\\n    \\n    return output, final_state\\n\"\n}\n","numLines":119,"startLine":1,"totalLines":119}}}
