{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Yb6VXeuHNxBe5RDsUZie2d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Executable Plan — L2/043 Mamba-2 Chunk Scan with Segment Sum\n2\t\n3\tCompanion to `docs/draft.md`. This file is the *executable* optimization plan:\n4\tconcrete kernel decomposition, sequential candidate lineage, correctness checks,\n5\tperformance hypotheses, stopping criteria, and evidence format. No candidate is\n6\timplemented or evaluated in this step.\n7\t\n8\tTarget: NVIDIA **A800 / sm_80 (Ampere)**, 108 SMs. Compute must be **Triton**;\n9\tPyTorch only for shapes/allocation/launch. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension\n10\tcomputational fallback. Ranking metric: **geometric-mean speedup** vs the fp32\n11\treference, gated on every selected workload passing correctness.\n12\t\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## 0. Fixed facts to build against\n16\t\n17\tConstants (from `task/definition.json`): `H=16`, `P=head_dim=64`, `N=state_size=256`,\n18\t`n_groups=1`, `Q=chunk_size=256`. Variable: `B=batch_size`, `L=seq_len`.\n19\tBecause `n_groups=1`, `B`/`C` params are **shared across all 16 heads**.\n20\t\n21\tFeedback workloads (fixed five; one immutable kernel over all five = one evaluation):\n22\t\n23\t| # | B | L | pad | L_pad | NC | last-chunk valid rows | (b,nc,h) tiles |\n24\t|---|---|-----|-----|-------|----|-----------------------|----------------|\n25\t| 1 | 2 | 293 | 219 | 512   | 2  | 37                    | 64  |\n26\t| 2 | 4 | 1024| 0   | 1024  | 4  | 256                   | 256 |\n27\t| 3 | 4 | 256 | 0   | 256   | 1  | 256                   | 64  |\n28\t| 4 | 1 | 1024| 0   | 1024  | 4  | 256                   | 64  |\n29\t| 5 | 4 | 541 | 227 | 768   | 3  | 29                    | 192 |\n30\t\n31\tPer-workload tolerance (all `max_rtol=0.05`, `required_match_ratio=0.98`):\n32\t`max_atol` = 0.020 / 0.029 / 0.024 / 0.022 / 0.029 for workloads 1..5.\n33\t`NC ≤ 4` in every case — inter-chunk recurrence is tiny. Parallel tiles 64–256, so\n34\t**occupancy is a first-order concern** for the 64-tile workloads (1, 3, 4).\n35\t\n36\tEntry point contract: `solution/solution.py` must expose\n37\t`run(hidden_states, A, B, C, D, initial_states) -> (output, final_state)` with\n38\t`output: [B, L, H*P] bf16` and `final_state: [B, H, P, N] bf16`, matching the\n39\treference signature exactly. Do not import or replicate the evaluator.\n40\t\n41\t---\n42\t\n43\t## 1. Reference algebra we implement (condensed from draft §2)\n44\t\n45\tPer `(b, chunk c, head h)`, with `a[t] = cumsum_t A` (fp32) over the chunk, `a_last=a[Q-1]`:\n46\t\n47\t- **Diagonal / intra-chunk:** `L[i,j] = exp(a[i]-a[j])` for `i≥j` else 0 (lower-tri).\n48\t  `G[i,j] = Σ_s C[i,s]·B[j,s]` (**head-independent**). `M = G∘L`.\n49\t  `Y_diag[i,:] = Σ_{j≤i} M[i,j]·X[j,:]` → `[Q,P]`.\n50\t- **Per-chunk state (right factor):** `decay_state[t]=exp(a_last-a[t])`;\n51\t  `states[p,n] = Σ_t decay_state[t]·X[t,p]·B[t,n]` → `[P,N]`.\n52\t- **Inter-chunk (middle):** `A_end_pad = [0, a_last(0..NC-1)]`,\n53\t  `decay_chunk = exp(segsum(A_end_pad))` `[NC+1,NC+1]`; recurrence over\n54\t  `states_with_init = concat(initial_states, states)`:\n55\t  `new_states[i] = Σ_{j≤i} decay_chunk[i,j]·states_with_init[j]`.\n56\t  `states_out = new_states[:-1]`, `final_state = new_states[-1]`.\n57\t- **Off-diagonal / state→out (left):** `Y_off[t,:] = exp(a[t])·Σ_s C[t,s]·states_out[s,:]` → `[Q,P]`.\n58\t- **Combine:** `y = Y_diag + Y_off`; reshape `[B,L_pad,H,P]`; add `D_residual=D[h]·X`;\n59\t  crop to `L`; reshape `[B,L,H*P]`; cast bf16. `final_state` cast bf16.\n60\t\n61\tKey exploitable levers: never materialize `[Q,Q]`/`[Q,Q,H]` to DRAM (the reference's\n62\tmulti-GB intermediates are the headroom); compute head-independent `G` once per group;\n63\t`NC≤4` ⇒ trivial recurrence; `N=Q=256`, `P=64` are matmul-friendly tiles.\n64\t\n65\t**Numerical fidelity invariants (must hold in every candidate):**\n66\t1. Always form the difference *then* exponentiate (`exp(a[i]-a[j])`, `exp(a_last-a[t])`,\n67\t   `exp(a[t])`) — never `exp(a[i])/exp(a[j])`.\n68\t2. Keep `A`, cumsum, all exp arguments, and all reductions in **fp32**; cast to bf16\n69\t   only when writing `output` and `final_state`.\n70\t3. Load padded/out-of-range seq rows with `other=0.0` (A-pad=0 keeps cumsum flat;\n71\t   X/B/C-pad=0 contribute nothing). Mask output stores to `< L`.\n72\t4. Add `D_residual` in fp32 before the final bf16 cast.\n73\t5. Respect the transposed layout of `A: [B,H,L]` vs seq-major `X/B/C: [B,L,·]`.\n74\t\n75\t---\n76\t\n77\t## 2. Kernel decomposition (Option A — faithful 4-stage SSD)\n78\t\n79\t`c001` implements Option A; later candidates fuse/tune. Working intermediates in fp32.\n80\t\n81\t- **K1 `chunk_cumsum`** — grid `(B, H, NC)` (or fold into K2). Loads `A[b,h,chunk]`\n82\t  with row mask, `a = tl.cumsum(A_row)` along Q. Emits `a: [B,H,NC,Q]` and\n83\t  `a_last: [B,H,NC]`. (Alternative: recompute inside K2/K4 to save a DRAM round-trip —\n84\t  a tuning axis, not for c001.)\n85\t- **K2 `chunk_state`** — grid over `(b, nc, h)`. Accumulate\n86\t  `states[p,n] = Σ_t exp(a_last-a[t])·X[t,p]·B[t,n]` with an fp32 `[P=64,N=256]`\n87\t  accumulator via `tl.dot` (reduce over t in blocks of Q). Emit `states: [B,NC,H,P,N]` fp32.\n88\t- **K3 `state_passing`** — grid `(B, H)`. Sequential recurrence over `c=0..NC-1`\n89\t  carrying an fp32 `[P,N]` state seeded by `initial_states`, applying per-chunk decay\n90\t  `g_c=exp(a_last(c))` exactly as `decay_chunk`. Emit `states_out: [B,NC,H,P,N]` fp32 and\n91\t  `final_state: [B,H,P,N]`. (NC≤4 ⇒ trivial; can also build the `[NC+1,NC+1]` matrix and\n92\t  do a small matmul — equivalent, pick the clearer one at implement time.)\n93\t- **K4 `chunk_scan`** — grid over `(b, nc, h[, i-block])`. Recompute/reload `a`. Compute\n94\t  `G=C·Bᵀ` (`tl.dot [Q,N]·[N,Q] → [Q,Q]`), apply lower-tri mask + `exp(a_i-a_j)` in\n95\t  registers → `M`; `Y_diag = M·X` (`[Q,Q]·[Q,P]`). Compute\n96\t  `Y_off = exp(a[t]) ⊙ (C·states_out)` (`[Q,N]·[N,P]`). `y=Y_diag+Y_off + D[h]·X`.\n97\t  Store to `output[b, c*Q + i, h*P : h*P+P]` masked to `< L`.\n98\t\n99\t**Layout/stride checklist for K1–K4** (verify statically before every eval, draft §4.8):\n100\t`A` is head-major `[B,H,L]`; `X` is `[B,L,H,P]`; `B/C` are `[B,L,1,N]` (group broadcast to\n101\tall H). Chunk c, row i ⇒ seq index `c*Q+i`; guard `< L` (and `< L_pad` implicitly by grid).\n102\t\n103\t**Alternative decompositions (later candidates, see §4):**\n104\t- Option B: two fused kernels (state stages 1–2; scan stages 1,3,4) — fewer launches,\n105\t  less intermediate DRAM.\n106\t- Option C: single mega-kernel per `(b,h)` looping chunks internally, carrying state in\n107\t  registers/SRAM (exploits NC≤4) — removes K3 and the `states` DRAM round-trip; higher\n108\t  register pressure, serializes chunks within a block.\n109\t\n110\t---\n111\t\n112\t## 3. Candidate lineage strategy\n113\t\n114\tRules: candidates are **immutable and sequential** (`c001`, `c002`, …); any meaningful\n115\tsource/config/launch change ⇒ new ID; never reuse an ID for changed source; append one\n116\tJSON record per evaluated candidate to `candidates.jsonl` (never rewrite). Each candidate\n117\tchanges **one primary variable** so the evaluation attributes cause→effect. Lineage is a\n118\ttree: every candidate names its `parent`; a regressed/failed candidate is abandoned and the\n119\tnext candidate branches from the last *known-good* parent (kept in `solution/solution.py`\n120\tonly when it is the current best).\n121\t\n122\tBranching policy:\n123\t- **Correctness first:** `c001` prioritizes passing all five workloads over peak speed\n124\t  (conservative precision). Do not start speed tuning until a passing baseline exists.\n125\t- **One knob per step:** precision mode, then head-shared `G` reuse, then fusion, then\n126\t  tiling/occupancy, then launch/dtype micro-tuning.\n127\t- **Keep-if-better:** accept a candidate as the new parent only if it (a) passes all five\n128\t  workloads and (b) improves geomean (or ties geomean with lower risk/complexity). Otherwise\n129\t  revert to the parent source for the next candidate.\n130\t- **Budget-aware:** 100-eval hard cap; token soft 1.0M / normal 1.5M / absolute 1.65M.\n131\t  Prefer high-information candidates; avoid speculative micro-sweeps once converged.\n132\t\n133\t### 3.1 Planned sequence (each = one evaluation; later steps are hypotheses, revised by evidence)\n134\t\n135\t- **c001 — Correct Option-A baseline.** 4 kernels (K1–K4), fp32 accumulation everywhere,\n136\t  `input_precision=\"ieee\"` on the exp-feeding matmuls (`C·Bᵀ`, `C·states`) and on\n137\t  `X·B`; `M·X` value matmul may use tf32 but start with ieee to lock correctness. Simple\n138\t  grid = one program per `(b,nc,h)` (plus per-`(b,h)` K3, per-`(b,h,nc)` K1). Goal: all\n139\t  five pass; establish baseline geomean.\n140\t- **c002 — Precision relaxation (tf32).** Parent c001. Switch the large matmuls to\n141\t  `input_precision=\"tf32\"` (rtol=0.05 is loose). Hypothesis: sizeable speedup, still\n142\t  passing. If any workload drops <0.98 match ratio, keep ieee selectively on the offending\n143\t  matmul only (that selective variant becomes its own candidate).\n144\t- **c003 — bf16 matmul inputs.** Parent = best of c001/c002. Feed `tl.dot` bf16-loaded\n145\t  X/B/C with fp32 accumulate for the value/score matmuls. Hypothesis: faster loads + tensor\n146\t  cores; watch exp-amplified paths (`C·Bᵀ`, `C·states`) — keep those higher-precision if\n147\t  they fail.\n148\t- **c004 — Head-shared G / group reuse.** Parent = best so far. Compute head-independent\n149\t  `G=C·Bᵀ` (and the `X·B`, `C·states` group contractions where legal) once per `(b,chunk)`\n150\t  and reuse across 16 heads (loop heads inside the block or restructure grid group-major).\n151\t  Hypothesis: up to ~16× less CB matmul work in K4 → large speedup on the compute-heavy\n152\t  workloads (2, 5).\n153\t- **c005 — Fuse cumsum (drop K1).** Recompute `a` inside K2/K4 instead of a separate\n154\t  kernel + DRAM round-trip. Hypothesis: fewer launches / less DRAM; neutral-to-positive.\n155\t- **c006 — Occupancy for 64-tile workloads.** Parent = best. Add an `i`-block (query) split\n156\t  and/or head/`P`/`N` splitting in K2/K4 to expose ≥108 concurrent blocks for workloads\n157\t  1/3/4. Tune `num_warps`∈{4,8}, `num_stages`∈{2,3,4}, `BLOCK_i`∈{64,128,256}. Hypothesis:\n158\t  fills SMs on the low-parallelism cases without hurting the 256-tile case.\n159\t- **c007 — Fusion (Option B/C).** Parent = best. Either merge K2/K4 into two kernels\n160\t  (Option B) or a per-`(b,h)` chunk-looping mega-kernel carrying state in registers\n161\t  (Option C, removing K3 + `states` round-trip). Hypothesis: removes DRAM traffic and\n162\t  launch overhead; risk = register pressure/occupancy. Adopt only if it beats c006.\n163\t- **c008+ — Micro-tuning.** Remaining knobs: tile shapes, `num_warps`/`num_stages` per\n164\t  kernel, `tl.dot` accumulator layout, vectorized/coalesced load ordering for the\n165\t  transposed `A`, optional `tl.exp2`-based decay (multiply cumsum by log2e) if faster and\n166\t  still in tolerance. One knob per candidate; stop when converged (§6).\n167\t\n168\tThis is a *plan*, not a script: after each evaluation, the observed per-workload\n169\tpass/fail + speedups redirect the next candidate (e.g. if tf32 already passes cleanly,\n170\tskip straight to head-shared reuse; if occupancy is the bottleneck, prioritize c006).\n171\t\n172\t---\n173\t\n174\t## 4. Correctness checks\n175\t\n176\t### 4.1 Static self-checks (no GPU) — run before *every* evaluation\n177\tRe-derive against draft §2/§4 and §1 above:\n178\t1. Shapes/strides for all loads/stores, including transposed `A: [B,H,L]` vs seq-major\n179\t   `X/B/C`, and `B/C` group broadcast (n_groups=1 → all 16 heads).\n180\t2. Seq index math `c*Q+i`; every seq-indexed load masked with `other=0.0`; every output\n181\t   store masked to `< L` (and final chunk’s valid-row count for workloads 1/5).\n182\t3. cumsum axis is the in-chunk Q axis and matches `torch.cumsum`; padded A rows load 0.\n183\t4. Exp-difference ordering (invariant §1.1) everywhere; all exp args fp32 (invariant §1.2).\n184\t5. `states_with_init` ordering (index 0 = initial_states), `states_out=new_states[:-1]`,\n185\t   `final_state=new_states[-1]`.\n186\t6. `D_residual` added in fp32 pre-cast; final bf16 casts on `output` and `final_state`.\n187\t7. Kernel launch grid covers all `(b,nc,h[,i])`; no out-of-bounds program IDs.\n188\t8. Output dtype/shape exactly `[B,L,H*P] bf16` and `[B,H,P,N] bf16`.\n189\t\n190\t### 4.2 Harness correctness (the only correctness signal)\n191\t`./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` over the five fixed workloads. Per\n192\tworkload: `atol ≤ max_atol`, `rtol ≤ 0.05`, fraction-within-tolerance `≥ 0.98`, on **both**\n193\t`output` and `final_state`. A candidate is **valid** only if all five pass; an invalid\n194\tcandidate cannot be a parent for speed claims. Never run CUDA/profilers/nvidia-smi/the\n195\texternal evaluator or any alternate correctness harness directly.\n196\t\n197\t### 4.3 Coverage argument\n198\tThe five workloads already span: heavy-padding partial last chunk (1: 37 valid rows; 5: 29),\n199\texact multiples (3: single chunk; 2/4: four chunks), and batch/occupancy variety\n200\t(B=1,2,4 → 64–256 tiles). Passing all five is a strong joint signal for masking/cumsum/decay\n201\tcorrectness and speed generality.\n202\t\n203\t### 4.4 Failure handling\n204\tA failed/erroring Triton kernel is **invalid** — fix the kernel; never substitute a\n205\tTorch/CPU/NumPy path. If a precision-relaxation candidate fails match ratio on a subset of\n206\tworkloads, the next candidate re-tightens precision only on the implicated matmul(s), as a\n207\tnew ID. Record the failure and its diagnosis in `candidates.jsonl`; do not silently retry\n208\tthe same source under a new ID.\n209\t\n210\t---\n211\t\n212\t## 5. Performance hypotheses\n213\t\n214\tRanked by expected impact (validated/refuted by evaluations):\n215\t\n216\t1. **Eliminating DRAM intermediates dominates.** The reference writes multi-GB\n217\t   `L:[B,H,NC,Q,Q]` and `G,M:[B,NC,Q,Q,H]` to DRAM; the op is memory-bound there. A fused\n218\t   Triton kernel that never materializes `[Q,Q]` should yield the largest speedup even\n219\t   before precision tuning. → biggest gain expected at c001→(fused) already.\n220\t2. **Head-shared `G` (n_groups=1)** removes ~16× redundant `C·Bᵀ` work in the diagonal\n221\t   stage → strong gains on compute-heavy workloads 2 and 5. (c004)\n222\t3. **tf32/bf16 matmul inputs** cut matmul latency with rtol=0.05 headroom; expected\n223\t   moderate uniform speedup if tolerance holds. (c002/c003)\n224\t4. **Occupancy** governs workloads 1/3/4 (64 tiles < 108 SMs): exposing extra grid\n225\t   parallelism (i-block / P / N splits) should lift those specifically without hurting the\n226\t   256-tile workload 2. (c006)\n227\t5. **Launch/round-trip reduction** (fuse cumsum, Option B/C) trims fixed overhead —\n228\t   relatively larger for the small workloads (1/3). (c005/c007)\n229\t6. **Micro-tuning** (`num_warps`/`num_stages`/tile shapes, `exp2` decay) — small,\n230\t   workload-dependent; only after structure is settled. (c008+)\n231\t\n232\tMetric definition: per-workload speedup = reference_time / candidate_time (as reported by\n233\tthe harness); primary score = **geometric mean of the five speedups**, reported only when\n234\tall five pass. Track both geomean and the per-workload vector to catch regressions masked by\n235\tthe mean (esp. the low-occupancy trio 1/3/4 vs compute-heavy 2/5).\n236\t\n237\t---\n238\t\n239\t## 6. Stopping criteria\n240\t\n241\tStop and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` (with reason) when any of:\n242\t1. **Convergence:** two or three consecutive accepted candidates improve geomean by\n243\t   < ~2% each, and the remaining ideas in §3 are exhausted or judged sub-2% — i.e.\n244\t   marginal returns below noise.\n245\t2. **Budget:** approaching the 100-evaluation cap, or the token **soft limit (1.0M)** with\n246\t   a valid best candidate in hand (hard-stop before 1.5M normal / 1.65M absolute).\n247\t3. **Structural ceiling:** the kernel is compute/occupancy-bound with no untried lever\n248\t   expected to matter on these five shapes (confirmed by the per-workload speedup pattern,\n249\t   reasoned — not by running a profiler here).\n250\t4. **No valid candidate risk:** if correctness cannot be achieved within tolerance after\n251\t   focused effort, keep the best *valid* candidate as the answer and stop tuning speed.\n252\t\n253\tAt stop: ensure `solution/solution.py` holds the best **valid** candidate’s source, the\n254\t`candidates.jsonl` lineage is complete, and `SEARCH_COMPLETE` states the reason and the\n255\tselected candidate ID. Never run `final` (16-workload) without explicit operator approval.\n256\t\n257\t---\n258\t\n259\t## 7. Evidence format\n260\t\n261\tOne JSON object appended to `candidates.jsonl` per evaluated candidate (never rewrite prior\n262\trecords). Schema:\n263\t\n264\t```json\n265\t{\n266\t  \"candidate_id\": \"c001\",\n267\t  \"parent\": null,\n268\t  \"source_sha256\": \"<hash of solution/solution.py at eval time>\",\n269\t  \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-17THH:MM:SSZ\",\n270\t  \"hypothesis\": \"Faithful Option-A 4-kernel SSD, fp32/ieee — establish passing baseline.\",\n271\t  \"change_from_parent\": \"initial implementation\",\n272\t  \"validation\": {\n273\t    \"static_checks\": \"pass  (strides/masks/cumsum/exp-order/dtypes verified vs plan §4.1)\",\n274\t    \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"\n275\t  },\n276\t  \"per_workload\": [\n277\t    {\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-...\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293,  \"passed\": true, \"max_atol_used\": 0.020,\n278\t     \"match_ratio_output\": null, \"match_ratio_final_state\": null, \"speedup\": null},\n279\t    {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-...\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": null},\n280\t    {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-...\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256,  \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": null},\n281\t    {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-...\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": null},\n282\t    {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-...\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541,  \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": null}\n283\t  ],\n284\t  \"all_passed\": true,\n285\t  \"geomean_speedup\": null,\n286\t  \"decision\": \"accept|reject|revert\",\n287\t  \"decision_reason\": \"baseline established / regressed on WL1 / correctness fail on WL5 ...\",\n288\t  \"cumulative_evaluations\": 1,\n289\t  \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere, out of skill scope)\"},\n290\t  \"notes\": \"observations, next-candidate direction\"\n291\t}\n292\t```\n293\t\n294\tFill `speedup`/`match_ratio`/`geomean` from the harness output for each real evaluation\n295\t(placeholders `null` here only because no candidate is evaluated in this step). Keep a\n296\trunning `cumulative_evaluations` counter. Record `decision` (accept and promote to parent,\n297\treject-and-branch-from-parent, or revert) with a one-line reason, and the per-candidate\n298\tskill usage honestly.\n299\t\n300\t---\n301\t\n302\t## 8. Risk register (carry from draft §8; resolve during search)\n303\t\n304\t1. Distribution/scale of `random` bf16 inputs → how often `exp` blows up; whether tf32/bf16\n305\t   matmul inputs stay within tolerance. Resolve empirically at c001/c002.\n306\t2. Best precision per matmul (ieee vs tf32 vs bf16-input) balancing speed vs 0.98 match.\n307\t3. Whether head-shared `G` reuse pays off vs. simpler per-head recompute.\n308\t4. Option A vs C for inter-chunk state given NC≤4 (register-carry mega-kernel removing a\n309\t   DRAM round-trip).\n310\t5. Occupancy tuning for the 64-tile workloads (1/3/4) on 108 SMs.\n311\t6. Exact stride handling for `A:[B,H,L]` vs seq-major X/B/C during chunking (static-check\n312\t   every candidate).\n313\t\n314\t---\n315\t\n316\t## 9. Immediate next action (next turn, not now)\n317\t\n318\tImplement `c001` = the correct Option-A 4-kernel Triton solution (fp32/ieee, simple grid),\n319\trun static self-checks (§4.1), then evaluate once via\n320\t`./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback c001` and append its record to\n321\t`candidates.jsonl`. No code is written or evaluated in this planning step.\n322\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"093a25e1-14b7-4d5e-9ce6-82fe02fef426","uuid":"4f7bebd6-823c-4486-b6f5-813d1a40eda6","timestamp":"2026-09-17T11:58:20.081Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-043_mamba_chunk_scan_with_segsum/docs/plan.md","content":"# Executable Plan — L2/043 Mamba-2 Chunk Scan with Segment Sum\n\nCompanion to `docs/draft.md`. This file is the *executable* optimization plan:\nconcrete kernel decomposition, sequential candidate lineage, correctness checks,\nperformance hypotheses, stopping criteria, and evidence format. No candidate is\nimplemented or evaluated in this step.\n\nTarget: NVIDIA **A800 / sm_80 (Ampere)**, 108 SMs. Compute must be **Triton**;\nPyTorch only for shapes/allocation/launch. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension\ncomputational fallback. Ranking metric: **geometric-mean speedup** vs the fp32\nreference, gated on every selected workload passing correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Fixed facts to build against\n\nConstants (from `task/definition.json`): `H=16`, `P=head_dim=64`, `N=state_size=256`,\n`n_groups=1`, `Q=chunk_size=256`. Variable: `B=batch_size`, `L=seq_len`.\nBecause `n_groups=1`, `B`/`C` params are **shared across all 16 heads**.\n\nFeedback workloads (fixed five; one immutable kernel over all five = one evaluation):\n\n| # | B | L | pad | L_pad | NC | last-chunk valid rows | (b,nc,h) tiles |\n|---|---|-----|-----|-------|----|-----------------------|----------------|\n| 1 | 2 | 293 | 219 | 512   | 2  | 37                    | 64  |\n| 2 | 4 | 1024| 0   | 1024  | 4  | 256                   | 256 |\n| 3 | 4 | 256 | 0   | 256   | 1  | 256                   | 64  |\n| 4 | 1 | 1024| 0   | 1024  | 4  | 256                   | 64  |\n| 5 | 4 | 541 | 227 | 768   | 3  | 29                    | 192 |\n\nPer-workload tolerance (all `max_rtol=0.05`, `required_match_ratio=0.98`):\n`max_atol` = 0.020 / 0.029 / 0.024 / 0.022 / 0.029 for workloads 1..5.\n`NC ≤ 4` in every case — inter-chunk recurrence is tiny. Parallel tiles 64–256, so\n**occupancy is a first-order concern** for the 64-tile workloads (1, 3, 4).\n\nEntry point contract: `solution/solution.py` must expose\n`run(hidden_states, A, B, C, D, initial_states) -> (output, final_state)` with\n`output: [B, L, H*P] bf16` and `final_state: [B, H, P, N] bf16`, matching the\nreference signature exactly. Do not import or replicate the evaluator.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Reference algebra we implement (condensed from draft §2)\n\nPer `(b, chunk c, head h)`, with `a[t] = cumsum_t A` (fp32) over the chunk, `a_last=a[Q-1]`:\n\n- **Diagonal / intra-chunk:** `L[i,j] = exp(a[i]-a[j])` for `i≥j` else 0 (lower-tri).\n  `G[i,j] = Σ_s C[i,s]·B[j,s]` (**head-independent**). `M = G∘L`.\n  `Y_diag[i,:] = Σ_{j≤i} M[i,j]·X[j,:]` → `[Q,P]`.\n- **Per-chunk state (right factor):** `decay_state[t]=exp(a_last-a[t])`;\n  `states[p,n] = Σ_t decay_state[t]·X[t,p]·B[t,n]` → `[P,N]`.\n- **Inter-chunk (middle):** `A_end_pad = [0, a_last(0..NC-1)]`,\n  `decay_chunk = exp(segsum(A_end_pad))` `[NC+1,NC+1]`; recurrence over\n  `states_with_init = concat(initial_states, states)`:\n  `new_states[i] = Σ_{j≤i} decay_chunk[i,j]·states_with_init[j]`.\n  `states_out = new_states[:-1]`, `final_state = new_states[-1]`.\n- **Off-diagonal / state→out (left):** `Y_off[t,:] = exp(a[t])·Σ_s C[t,s]·states_out[s,:]` → `[Q,P]`.\n- **Combine:** `y = Y_diag + Y_off`; reshape `[B,L_pad,H,P]`; add `D_residual=D[h]·X`;\n  crop to `L`; reshape `[B,L,H*P]`; cast bf16. `final_state` cast bf16.\n\nKey exploitable levers: never materialize `[Q,Q]`/`[Q,Q,H]` to DRAM (the reference's\nmulti-GB intermediates are the headroom); compute head-independent `G` once per group;\n`NC≤4` ⇒ trivial recurrence; `N=Q=256`, `P=64` are matmul-friendly tiles.\n\n**Numerical fidelity invariants (must hold in every candidate):**\n1. Always form the difference *then* exponentiate (`exp(a[i]-a[j])`, `exp(a_last-a[t])`,\n   `exp(a[t])`) — never `exp(a[i])/exp(a[j])`.\n2. Keep `A`, cumsum, all exp arguments, and all reductions in **fp32**; cast to bf16\n   only when writing `output` and `final_state`.\n3. Load padded/out-of-range seq rows with `other=0.0` (A-pad=0 keeps cumsum flat;\n   X/B/C-pad=0 contribute nothing). Mask output stores to `< L`.\n4. Add `D_residual` in fp32 before the final bf16 cast.\n5. Respect the transposed layout of `A: [B,H,L]` vs seq-major `X/B/C: [B,L,·]`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Kernel decomposition (Option A — faithful 4-stage SSD)\n\n`c001` implements Option A; later candidates fuse/tune. Working intermediates in fp32.\n\n- **K1 `chunk_cumsum`** — grid `(B, H, NC)` (or fold into K2). Loads `A[b,h,chunk]`\n  with row mask, `a = tl.cumsum(A_row)` along Q. Emits `a: [B,H,NC,Q]` and\n  `a_last: [B,H,NC]`. (Alternative: recompute inside K2/K4 to save a DRAM round-trip —\n  a tuning axis, not for c001.)\n- **K2 `chunk_state`** — grid over `(b, nc, h)`. Accumulate\n  `states[p,n] = Σ_t exp(a_last-a[t])·X[t,p]·B[t,n]` with an fp32 `[P=64,N=256]`\n  accumulator via `tl.dot` (reduce over t in blocks of Q). Emit `states: [B,NC,H,P,N]` fp32.\n- **K3 `state_passing`** — grid `(B, H)`. Sequential recurrence over `c=0..NC-1`\n  carrying an fp32 `[P,N]` state seeded by `initial_states`, applying per-chunk decay\n  `g_c=exp(a_last(c))` exactly as `decay_chunk`. Emit `states_out: [B,NC,H,P,N]` fp32 and\n  `final_state: [B,H,P,N]`. (NC≤4 ⇒ trivial; can also build the `[NC+1,NC+1]` matrix and\n  do a small matmul — equivalent, pick the clearer one at implement time.)\n- **K4 `chunk_scan`** — grid over `(b, nc, h[, i-block])`. Recompute/reload `a`. Compute\n  `G=C·Bᵀ` (`tl.dot [Q,N]·[N,Q] → [Q,Q]`), apply lower-tri mask + `exp(a_i-a_j)` in\n  registers → `M`; `Y_diag = M·X` (`[Q,Q]·[Q,P]`). Compute\n  `Y_off = exp(a[t]) ⊙ (C·states_out)` (`[Q,N]·[N,P]`). `y=Y_diag+Y_off + D[h]·X`.\n  Store to `output[b, c*Q + i, h*P : h*P+P]` masked to `< L`.\n\n**Layout/stride checklist for K1–K4** (verify statically before every eval, draft §4.8):\n`A` is head-major `[B,H,L]`; `X` is `[B,L,H,P]`; `B/C` are `[B,L,1,N]` (group broadcast to\nall H). Chunk c, row i ⇒ seq index `c*Q+i`; guard `< L` (and `< L_pad` implicitly by grid).\n\n**Alternative decompositions (later candidates, see §4):**\n- Option B: two fused kernels (state stages 1–2; scan stages 1,3,4) — fewer launches,\n  less intermediate DRAM.\n- Option C: single mega-kernel per `(b,h)` looping chunks internally, carrying state in\n  registers/SRAM (exploits NC≤4) — removes K3 and the `states` DRAM round-trip; higher\n  register pressure, serializes chunks within a block.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Candidate lineage strategy\n\nRules: candidates are **immutable and sequential** (`c001`, `c002`, …); any meaningful\nsource/config/launch change ⇒ new ID; never reuse an ID for changed source; append one\nJSON record per evaluated candidate to `candidates.jsonl` (never rewrite). Each candidate\nchanges **one primary variable** so the evaluation attributes cause→effect. Lineage is a\ntree: every candidate names its `parent`; a regressed/failed candidate is abandoned and the\nnext candidate branches from the last *known-good* parent (kept in `solution/solution.py`\nonly when it is the current best).\n\nBranching policy:\n- **Correctness first:** `c001` prioritizes passing all five workloads over peak speed\n  (conservative precision). Do not start speed tuning until a passing baseline exists.\n- **One knob per step:** precision mode, then head-shared `G` reuse, then fusion, then\n  tiling/occupancy, then launch/dtype micro-tuning.\n- **Keep-if-better:** accept a candidate as the new parent only if it (a) passes all five\n  workloads and (b) improves geomean (or ties geomean with lower risk/complexity). Otherwise\n  revert to the parent source for the next candidate.\n- **Budget-aware:** 100-eval hard cap; token soft 1.0M / normal 1.5M / absolute 1.65M.\n  Prefer high-information candidates; avoid speculative micro-sweeps once converged.\n\n### 3.1 Planned sequence (each = one evaluation; later steps are hypotheses, revised by evidence)\n\n- **c001 — Correct Option-A baseline.** 4 kernels (K1–K4), fp32 accumulation everywhere,\n  `input_precision=\"ieee\"` on the exp-feeding matmuls (`C·Bᵀ`, `C·states`) and on\n  `X·B`; `M·X` value matmul may use tf32 but start with ieee to lock correctness. Simple\n  grid = one program per `(b,nc,h)` (plus per-`(b,h)` K3, per-`(b,h,nc)` K1). Goal: all\n  five pass; establish baseline geomean.\n- **c002 — Precision relaxation (tf32).** Parent c001. Switch the large matmuls to\n  `input_precision=\"tf32\"` (rtol=0.05 is loose). Hypothesis: sizeable speedup, still\n  passing. If any workload drops <0.98 match ratio, keep ieee selectively on the offending\n  matmul only (that selective variant becomes its own candidate).\n- **c003 — bf16 matmul inputs.** Parent = best of c001/c002. Feed `tl.dot` bf16-loaded\n  X/B/C with fp32 accumulate for the value/score matmuls. Hypothesis: faster loads + tensor\n  cores; watch exp-amplified paths (`C·Bᵀ`, `C·states`) — keep those higher-precision if\n  they fail.\n- **c004 — Head-shared G / group reuse.** Parent = best so far. Compute head-independent\n  `G=C·Bᵀ` (and the `X·B`, `C·states` group contractions where legal) once per `(b,chunk)`\n  and reuse across 16 heads (loop heads inside the block or restructure grid group-major).\n  Hypothesis: up to ~16× less CB matmul work in K4 → large speedup on the compute-heavy\n  workloads (2, 5).\n- **c005 — Fuse cumsum (drop K1).** Recompute `a` inside K2/K4 instead of a separate\n  kernel + DRAM round-trip. Hypothesis: fewer launches / less DRAM; neutral-to-positive.\n- **c006 — Occupancy for 64-tile workloads.** Parent = best. Add an `i`-block (query) split\n  and/or head/`P`/`N` splitting in K2/K4 to expose ≥108 concurrent blocks for workloads\n  1/3/4. Tune `num_warps`∈{4,8}, `num_stages`∈{2,3,4}, `BLOCK_i`∈{64,128,256}. Hypothesis:\n  fills SMs on the low-parallelism cases without hurting the 256-tile case.\n- **c007 — Fusion (Option B/C).** Parent = best. Either merge K2/K4 into two kernels\n  (Option B) or a per-`(b,h)` chunk-looping mega-kernel carrying state in registers\n  (Option C, removing K3 + `states` round-trip). Hypothesis: removes DRAM traffic and\n  launch overhead; risk = register pressure/occupancy. Adopt only if it beats c006.\n- **c008+ — Micro-tuning.** Remaining knobs: tile shapes, `num_warps`/`num_stages` per\n  kernel, `tl.dot` accumulator layout, vectorized/coalesced load ordering for the\n  transposed `A`, optional `tl.exp2`-based decay (multiply cumsum by log2e) if faster and\n  still in tolerance. One knob per candidate; stop when converged (§6).\n\nThis is a *plan*, not a script: after each evaluation, the observed per-workload\npass/fail + speedups redirect the next candidate (e.g. if tf32 already passes cleanly,\nskip straight to head-shared reuse; if occupancy is the bottleneck, prioritize c006).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Correctness checks\n\n### 4.1 Static self-checks (no GPU) — run before *every* evaluation\nRe-derive against draft §2/§4 and §1 above:\n1. Shapes/strides for all loads/stores, including transposed `A: [B,H,L]` vs seq-major\n   `X/B/C`, and `B/C` group broadcast (n_groups=1 → all 16 heads).\n2. Seq index math `c*Q+i`; every seq-indexed load masked with `other=0.0`; every output\n   store masked to `< L` (and final chunk’s valid-row count for workloads 1/5).\n3. cumsum axis is the in-chunk Q axis and matches `torch.cumsum`; padded A rows load 0.\n4. Exp-difference ordering (invariant §1.1) everywhere; all exp args fp32 (invariant §1.2).\n5. `states_with_init` ordering (index 0 = initial_states), `states_out=new_states[:-1]`,\n   `final_state=new_states[-1]`.\n6. `D_residual` added in fp32 pre-cast; final bf16 casts on `output` and `final_state`.\n7. Kernel launch grid covers all `(b,nc,h[,i])`; no out-of-bounds program IDs.\n8. Output dtype/shape exactly `[B,L,H*P] bf16` and `[B,H,P,N] bf16`.\n\n### 4.2 Harness correctness (the only correctness signal)\n`./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN` over the five fixed workloads. Per\nworkload: `atol ≤ max_atol`, `rtol ≤ 0.05`, fraction-within-tolerance `≥ 0.98`, on **both**\n`output` and `final_state`. A candidate is **valid** only if all five pass; an invalid\ncandidate cannot be a parent for speed claims. Never run CUDA/profilers/nvidia-smi/the\nexternal evaluator or any alternate correctness harness directly.\n\n### 4.3 Coverage argument\nThe five workloads already span: heavy-padding partial last chunk (1: 37 valid rows; 5: 29),\nexact multiples (3: single chunk; 2/4: four chunks), and batch/occupancy variety\n(B=1,2,4 → 64–256 tiles). Passing all five is a strong joint signal for masking/cumsum/decay\ncorrectness and speed generality.\n\n### 4.4 Failure handling\nA failed/erroring Triton kernel is **invalid** — fix the kernel; never substitute a\nTorch/CPU/NumPy path. If a precision-relaxation candidate fails match ratio on a subset of\nworkloads, the next candidate re-tightens precision only on the implicated matmul(s), as a\nnew ID. Record the failure and its diagnosis in `candidates.jsonl`; do not silently retry\nthe same source under a new ID.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Performance hypotheses\n\nRanked by expected impact (validated/refuted by evaluations):\n\n1. **Eliminating DRAM intermediates dominates.** The reference writes multi-GB\n   `L:[B,H,NC,Q,Q]` and `G,M:[B,NC,Q,Q,H]` to DRAM; the op is memory-bound there. A fused\n   Triton kernel that never materializes `[Q,Q]` should yield the largest speedup even\n   before precision tuning. → biggest gain expected at c001→(fused) already.\n2. **Head-shared `G` (n_groups=1)** removes ~16× redundant `C·Bᵀ` work in the diagonal\n   stage → strong gains on compute-heavy workloads 2 and 5. (c004)\n3. **tf32/bf16 matmul inputs** cut matmul latency with rtol=0.05 headroom; expected\n   moderate uniform speedup if tolerance holds. (c002/c003)\n4. **Occupancy** governs workloads 1/3/4 (64 tiles < 108 SMs): exposing extra grid\n   parallelism (i-block / P / N splits) should lift those specifically without hurting the\n   256-tile workload 2. (c006)\n5. **Launch/round-trip reduction** (fuse cumsum, Option B/C) trims fixed overhead —\n   relatively larger for the small workloads (1/3). (c005/c007)\n6. **Micro-tuning** (`num_warps`/`num_stages`/tile shapes, `exp2` decay) — small,\n   workload-dependent; only after structure is settled. (c008+)\n\nMetric definition: per-workload speedup = reference_time / candidate_time (as reported by\nthe harness); primary score = **geometric mean of the five speedups**, reported only when\nall five pass. Track both geomean and the per-workload vector to catch regressions masked by\nthe mean (esp. the low-occupancy trio 1/3/4 vs compute-heavy 2/5).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Stopping criteria\n\nStop and write `SEARCH_COMPLETE` (with reason) when any of:\n1. **Convergence:** two or three consecutive accepted candidates improve geomean by\n   < ~2% each, and the remaining ideas in §3 are exhausted or judged sub-2% — i.e.\n   marginal returns below noise.\n2. **Budget:** approaching the 100-evaluation cap, or the token **soft limit (1.0M)** with\n   a valid best candidate in hand (hard-stop before 1.5M normal / 1.65M absolute).\n3. **Structural ceiling:** the kernel is compute/occupancy-bound with no untried lever\n   expected to matter on these five shapes (confirmed by the per-workload speedup pattern,\n   reasoned — not by running a profiler here).\n4. **No valid candidate risk:** if correctness cannot be achieved within tolerance after\n   focused effort, keep the best *valid* candidate as the answer and stop tuning speed.\n\nAt stop: ensure `solution/solution.py` holds the best **valid** candidate’s source, the\n`candidates.jsonl` lineage is complete, and `SEARCH_COMPLETE` states the reason and the\nselected candidate ID. Never run `final` (16-workload) without explicit operator approval.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Evidence format\n\nOne JSON object appended to `candidates.jsonl` per evaluated candidate (never rewrite prior\nrecords). Schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"candidate_id\": \"c001\",\n  \"parent\": null,\n  \"source_sha256\": \"<hash of solution/solution.py at eval time>\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-17THH:MM:SSZ\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Faithful Option-A 4-kernel SSD, fp32/ieee — establish passing baseline.\",\n  \"change_from_parent\": \"initial implementation\",\n  \"validation\": {\n    \"static_checks\": \"pass  (strides/masks/cumsum/exp-order/dtypes verified vs plan §4.1)\",\n    \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"\n  },\n  \"per_workload\": [\n    {\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-...\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293,  \"passed\": true, \"max_atol_used\": 0.020,\n     \"match_ratio_output\": null, \"match_ratio_final_state\": null, \"speedup\": null},\n    {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-...\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": null},\n    {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-...\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256,  \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": null},\n    {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-...\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": null},\n    {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-...\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541,  \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": null}\n  ],\n  \"all_passed\": true,\n  \"geomean_speedup\": null,\n  \"decision\": \"accept|reject|revert\",\n  \"decision_reason\": \"baseline established / regressed on WL1 / correctness fail on WL5 ...\",\n  \"cumulative_evaluations\": 1,\n  \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere, out of skill scope)\"},\n  \"notes\": \"observations, next-candidate direction\"\n}\n```\n\nFill `speedup`/`match_ratio`/`geomean` from the harness output for each real evaluation\n(placeholders `null` here only because no candidate is evaluated in this step). Keep a\nrunning `cumulative_evaluations` counter. Record `decision` (accept and promote to parent,\nreject-and-branch-from-parent, or revert) with a one-line reason, and the per-candidate\nskill usage honestly.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Risk register (carry from draft §8; resolve during search)\n\n1. Distribution/scale of `random` bf16 inputs → how often `exp` blows up; whether tf32/bf16\n   matmul inputs stay within tolerance. Resolve empirically at c001/c002.\n2. Best precision per matmul (ieee vs tf32 vs bf16-input) balancing speed vs 0.98 match.\n3. Whether head-shared `G` reuse pays off vs. simpler per-head recompute.\n4. Option A vs C for inter-chunk state given NC≤4 (register-carry mega-kernel removing a\n   DRAM round-trip).\n5. Occupancy tuning for the 64-tile workloads (1/3/4) on 108 SMs.\n6. Exact stride handling for `A:[B,H,L]` vs seq-major X/B/C during chunking (static-check\n   every candidate).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Immediate next action (next turn, not now)\n\nImplement `c001` = the correct Option-A 4-kernel Triton solution (fp32/ieee, simple grid),\nrun static self-checks (§4.1), then evaluate once via\n`./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback c001` and append its record to\n`candidates.jsonl`. No code is written or evaluated in this planning step.\n","numLines":322,"startLine":1,"totalLines":322}}}
