{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GgtiLJpCEcd7a1a1yPEaZc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"L2/043 Mamba-2 chunk scan with segment sum — Triton solution.\n2\t\n3\tCandidate c002: Option-A 4-stage SSD, bf16 tensor-core matmuls (fp32 accumulation).\n4\t\n5\tChange vs c001 (which RUNTIME_ERROR'd on all 5 workloads — a compile/launch failure,\n6\tnot algebra): every ``tl.dot`` now consumes **bf16 operands** with the default fp32\n7\taccumulator, and the ``input_precision=\"ieee\"`` kwarg is removed. This (a) sidesteps any\n8\tkwarg-compat issue on the installed Triton build and (b) halves dot-operand shared memory,\n9\twhich is the prime suspect for the c001 launch failure (fp32 [64,256]/[256,64] tiles in a\n10\tpipelined loop overflow A800 SRAM). ``num_warps``/``num_stages`` are pinned to bound SRAM.\n11\t\n12\tNumerical note: the input tensors are natively bf16, so bf16 tensor-core inputs carry the\n13\tsame operand precision as the reference (which merely upcasts bf16→fp32 losslessly before\n14\tmatmul); products accumulate in fp32. All decays, cumsum, exp arguments, accumulators and\n15\tthe D residual stay in fp32; only ``output``/``final_state`` are cast to bf16 at the end.\n16\t\n17\tStages (all working intermediates in fp32; only output / final_state cast to bf16):\n18\t  K1 chunk_cumsum   : a[t] = cumsum_t A over each chunk, plus a_last = a[Q-1].\n19\t  K2 chunk_state    : states[p,n] = sum_t exp(a_last - a[t]) * X[t,p] * B[t,n].\n20\t  K3 state_passing  : sequential inter-chunk recurrence seeded by initial_states,\n21\t                      new_states[c+1] = exp(a_last(c)) * new_states[c] + states[c];\n22\t                      states_out[c] = new_states[c], final_state = new_states[NC].\n23\t  K4 chunk_scan     : Y = (G∘L)·X  +  exp(a[t])·(C·states_out)  +  D[h]·X\n24\t                      with G[i,j] = C_i·B_j (head-independent), L[i,j]=exp(a[i]-a[j]),\n25\t                      lower-triangular; never materializes [Q,Q] to DRAM.\n26\t\n27\tConstants for this task: H=16, P=64, N=256, n_groups=1, Q(chunk)=256.\n28\tNo Torch/CPU/NumPy computational fallback anywhere: Triton is the only compute path.\n29\t\"\"\"\n30\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6fba2c0-4823-4806-9479-4c36036ed424","uuid":"7515528c-4a23-492c-9a46-4f96edeb5f54","timestamp":"2026-09-17T20:37:08.508Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-043_mamba_chunk_scan_with_segsum/solution/solution.py","content":"\"\"\"L2/043 Mamba-2 chunk scan with segment sum — Triton solution.\n\nCandidate c002: Option-A 4-stage SSD, bf16 tensor-core matmuls (fp32 accumulation).\n\nChange vs c001 (which RUNTIME_ERROR'd on all 5 workloads — a compile/launch failure,\nnot algebra): every ``tl.dot`` now consumes **bf16 operands** with the default fp32\naccumulator, and the ``input_precision=\"ieee\"`` kwarg is removed. This (a) sidesteps any\nkwarg-compat issue on the installed Triton build and (b) halves dot-operand shared memory,\nwhich is the prime suspect for the c001 launch failure (fp32 [64,256]/[256,64] tiles in a\npipelined loop overflow A800 SRAM). ``num_warps``/``num_stages`` are pinned to bound SRAM.\n\nNumerical note: the input tensors are natively bf16, so bf16 tensor-core inputs carry the\nsame operand precision as the reference (which merely upcasts bf16→fp32 losslessly before\nmatmul); products accumulate in fp32. All decays, cumsum, exp arguments, accumulators and\nthe D residual stay in fp32; only ``output``/``final_state`` are cast to bf16 at the end.\n\nStages (all working intermediates in fp32; only output / final_state cast to bf16):\n  K1 chunk_cumsum   : a[t] = cumsum_t A over each chunk, plus a_last = a[Q-1].\n  K2 chunk_state    : states[p,n] = sum_t exp(a_last - a[t]) * X[t,p] * B[t,n].\n  K3 state_passing  : sequential inter-chunk recurrence seeded by initial_states,\n                      new_states[c+1] = exp(a_last(c)) * new_states[c] + states[c];\n                      states_out[c] = new_states[c], final_state = new_states[NC].\n  K4 chunk_scan     : Y = (G∘L)·X  +  exp(a[t])·(C·states_out)  +  D[h]·X\n                      with G[i,j] = C_i·B_j (head-independent), L[i,j]=exp(a[i]-a[j]),\n                      lower-triangular; never materializes [Q,Q] to DRAM.\n\nConstants for this task: H=16, P=64, N=256, n_groups=1, Q(chunk)=256.\nNo Torch/CPU/NumPy computational fallback anywhere: Triton is the only compute path.\n\"\"\"\n","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":320}}}
