rm, non-tolerance). Kernel compile/launch failure, not algebra. Leading suspects: (a) tl.dot(input_precision='ieee') kwarg unsupported on the installed Triton build (older Triton uses allow_tf32=); (b) fp32 tl.dot on large [64,256]/[256,64] tiles overflowing A800 shared memory with default pipelining/num_warps=8. Harness exposes no traceback; could not probe triton version (bash python blocked by sandbox).\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 1, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Next c002: remove input_precision kwarg entirely and feed bf16 operands into every tl.dot (fp32 accumulation kept by default), which both sidesteps the kwarg-compat risk and halves dot-operand SRAM; keep decays/exp/cumsum/accumulators/D-residual in fp32; add explicit num_warps=4, num_stages=2 to bound SRAM. K1-K4 algebra unchanged (verified).\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c002\", \"parent\": \"c001\", \"source_sha256\": \"0b2cf934a0efdafdd94ec935835da2001dcdf74ae85e1eecb4985b5c41686293\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T00:30:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"c001 failed to launch (all RUNTIME_ERROR). Feed bf16 operands into every tl.dot with default fp32 accumulation and drop the input_precision kwarg; pin num_warps=4/num_stages=2. This removes kwarg-compat risk and halves dot-operand SRAM while keeping all decays/exp/cumsum/accumulators/D-residual in fp32. Expect all 5 to pass and a large speedup over the memory-bound reference.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"All tl.dot now consume bf16 operands (Xd cast to bf16 after fp32 decay in K2; M cast to bf16 after fp32 mask/exp in K4; states_o cast to bf16 for C.states dot); removed input_precision='ieee'; num_warps 8->4 on K2/K4, added num_stages=2 to K2/K4.\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (algebra identical to c001; only dot operand dtype + launch params changed)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 10.486993283989488, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.157534, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.9835205078125}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.25298535944318, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.254157, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 8.973383428660055, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.1579655, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 8.939661476559777, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.1593095, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 13.229428499147975, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.209501, \"max_abs\": 2.0, \"max_rel\": 5.1437907218933105}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 10.640015419344408, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 10.776490409560095, \"decision\": \"accept\", \"decision_reason\": \"All 5 workloads PASS; geomean 10.64x over the memory-bound reference. New best and parent. bf16 tl.dot operands confirmed the c001 failure was compilation/launch (kwarg or SRAM), not algebra. Correctness holds comfortably (bf16 operands == reference operand precision; fp32 accumulation).\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 2, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Occupancy pattern as predicted: 64-tile workloads (WL3 L=256 8.97x, WL4 B=1 8.94x) trail the high-parallelism ones (WL2 256-tile 12.25x, WL5 192-tile 13.23x). Next levers: (c003) head-shared G reuse in K4 -- compute C.B^T once per (b,chunk) across all 16 heads (n_groups=1) to cut the diagonal matmul ~16x, and/or expose more grid parallelism for the 64-tile cases.\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c003\", \"parent\": \"c002\", \"source_sha256\": \"e1384b72946ae8af7ec8f631a889e18370d690406d806776f4aaf2be6364e3e8\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T01:00:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"K4 recomputes the head-independent G = C.B^T 16x (once per head). Precompute G once per (b,chunk) in a new kernel Kg (fp32, lower-tri blocks) and load it in K4, cutting diagonal matmul FLOPs ~2x. Expect a speedup, especially on compute-heavy WL2/WL5.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"Added Kg kernel writing G:[B,NC,Q,Q] fp32; K4 loads G tile instead of computing tl.dot(C_i, B_j^T) and no longer loads B in the j-loop. Diagonal numerics preserved bit-for-bit (G fp32 * fp32 L, then bf16 for M.X). Kg grid (B*NC, Q/BLOCK_M).\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (G indexing im/jm over sG_i/sG_j; only lower-tri blocks written & read; algebra identical to c002 diagonal term)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 9.573956507251205, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.1725575, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.9835205078125}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.768247707569275, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.2439005, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 8.092261318581572, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.1751655, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 8.104532353016493, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.1757255, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.956326519163975, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.213917, \"max_abs\": 2.0, \"max_rel\": 5.1437907218933105}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 10.076294, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 10.299065, \"decision\": \"reject\", \"decision_reason\": \"5/5 PASS but geomean 10.08x REGRESSES vs c002's 10.64x (-5.3%). Splitting G into a separate kernel added a full [B,NC,Q,Q] fp32 DRAM round-trip (write in Kg + read in K4) plus an extra launch, which outweighs the saved matmul FLOPs. Compute-heavy WL2/WL5 improved slightly (12.25->12.77, 13.23->12.96 mixed) but the 64-tile WL1/WL3/WL4 got worse (10.49->9.57, 8.97->8.09, 8.94->8.10) because the added G traffic dominates when parallelism/compute is already low. Confirms c002 is NOT bound by redundant G matmul; the G recompute is essentially free relative to memory/launch overhead. Revert to c002.\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 3, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Lesson: materializing G to DRAM is a net loss; head-shared reuse only helps if kept in-SRAM (loop heads inside one K4 block so G stays resident). Next c004: keep c002 structure but fuse heads in K4 -- one program per (b,chunk,i-block) loops over all 16 heads, computing each G=C.B^T block ONCE and reusing across heads while X/states/decay vary per head. This keeps G in registers/SRAM (no DRAM round-trip) and cuts G-matmul FLOPs ~16x. Reverting solution.py to c002 source for the c004 branch.\"}\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":91480,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":4871,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6fba2c0-4823-4806-9479-4c36036ed424","uuid":"a2d00775-ae0e-4885-83f2-8e79b2a02812","timestamp":"2026-09-17T20:51:20.575Z"}
