r sG_i/sG_j; only lower-tri blocks written & read; algebra identical to c002 diagonal term)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 9.573956507251205, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.1725575, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.9835205078125}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.768247707569275, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.2439005, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 8.092261318581572, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.1751655, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 8.104532353016493, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.1757255, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.956326519163975, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.213917, \"max_abs\": 2.0, \"max_rel\": 5.1437907218933105}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 10.076294, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 10.299065, \"decision\": \"reject\", \"decision_reason\": \"5/5 PASS but geomean 10.08x REGRESSES vs c002's 10.64x (-5.3%). Splitting G into a separate kernel added a full [B,NC,Q,Q] fp32 DRAM round-trip (write in Kg + read in K4) plus an extra launch, which outweighs the saved matmul FLOPs. Compute-heavy WL2/WL5 improved slightly (12.25->12.77, 13.23->12.96 mixed) but the 64-tile WL1/WL3/WL4 got worse (10.49->9.57, 8.97->8.09, 8.94->8.10) because the added G traffic dominates when parallelism/compute is already low. Confirms c002 is 