 to DRAM hurts; (c004) inline cumsum recompute hurts and K1 is cheap. Both point to c002 being memory/launch-tuned already, NOT matmul- or K1-bound. The real large tensors are states/states_out:[B,NC,H,P,N] fp32 (~268MB each for WL2, ~1GB round-trip). Next c005: keep c002 structure but store states & states_out in bf16 to halve that traffic. Low risk numerically: K4 already casts states_out->bf16 before the C.states dot (so the off-diagonal term is bit-identical), and K3 accumulates the recurrence in fp32 with only the per-chunk increment rounded to bf16 (NC<=4 additions, well within rtol=0.05). Reverting solution.py to c002 for the c005 branch.\"}","+{\"candidate_id\": \"c005\", \"parent\": \"c002\", \"source_sha256\": \"4dbc6b46eeef0a575fb3892dedbf3585948fbf7ccf44cb0940e3ae507170ee6b\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T02:00:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"c002 is memory/launch-lean but still round-trips the large states/states_out:[B,NC,H,P,N] fp32 tensors (~0.27GB each on WL2). Store both in bf16 to halve that DRAM traffic; compute stays fp32. K4 already cast states_out->bf16 before the C.states dot (off-diagonal bit-identical); K3 recurrence stays fp32 in-register with only the per-chunk increment bf16-rounded (NC<=4 adds, <<rtol). Expect a uniform speedup.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"states and states_out tensors allocated bf16 (was fp32); K2 stores states.to(bf16); K3 stores state.to(bf16) for states_out and carries state in fp32 registers; K4 loads states_out as bf16 directly (removed the redundant .to(bf16)). All arithmetic still fp32.\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (only intermediate storage dtype changed; recurrence/decay/exp/accumulators all fp32; off-diagonal term numerically identical to c002)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 11.030339044159867, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.149774, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.9835205078125}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 14.21968448208945, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.219005, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 9.360167197136782, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.151438, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 9.37955584241099, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.151838, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 14.54705944101824, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.190525, \"max_abs\": 2.0, \"max_rel\": 5.1437907218933105}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 11.490600605016208, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 11.707361201, \"decision\": \"accept\", \"decision_reason\": \"5/5 PASS; geomean 11.49x, up from c002's 10.64x (+8%). New best and parent. Every workload improved (WL1 10.49->11.03, WL2 12.25->14.22, WL3 8.97->9.36, WL4 8.94->9.38, WL5 13.23->14.55). Confirms the states/states_out fp32 round-trip was a real bottleneck; bf16 storage halves it with no correctness loss (max_abs/max_rel identical to c002).\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 5, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Compute-heavy WL2/WL5 gained most (~+2 speedup), consistent with them having the largest states tensors. 64-tile WL3/WL4 still ~9.4x, launch/occupancy-limited. Next c006: attack the 64-tile floor by exposing more grid parallelism, OR try the acum tensor in bf16 too. Prefer the occupancy lever for WL3/WL4.\"}"]}],"userModified":fals