 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 16.796645182168767, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.185405, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.083240985423238, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.11731, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.148485249145912, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.1172305, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 17.774048635961368, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.155934, \"max_abs\": 2.0, \"max_rel\": 5.1437907218933105}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 14.451465, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 14.637067, \"decision\": \"accept\", \"decision_reason\": \"5/5 PASS; geomean 14.45x, up from c005's 11.49x (+26%). New best and parent. Every workload improved substantially (WL1 11.03->14.38, WL2 14.22->16.80, WL3 9.36->12.08, WL4 9.38->12.15, WL5 14.55->17.77). Folding K1 into K2 removed a full launch + A-read and the small workloads gained most (WL1 +30%, WL3/WL4 +29%), confirming the floor was launch/overhead-bound. max_abs/max_rel identical to c005 => numerics unchanged. Contrast with c004: recomputing cumsum in K4 was fatal, but computing it once in K2 and storing is a clear win.\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 6, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Now 3 launches (K2,K3,K4). K3 is tiny (grid B*H*4, NC<=4 sequential) and a candidate to fold into K2 or K4, but the inter-chunk recurrence is sequential over chunks so it cannot trivially merge with the per-(b,chunk) K2/K4. Next c007: try folding K3 into K4 via a single (b,h)-serial mega-kernel that loops chunks carrying state in registers (Option C) to drop another launch + the states/states_out round-trip; risk = serializing chunks & lower parallelism on WL3/WL4. Alternatively micro-tune num_warps/BLOCK_N on K2. c006 is a strong best; remaining gains likely smaller.\"}"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":158436,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":4871,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6fba2c0-4823-4806-9479-4c36036ed424","uuid":"fc4ed130-406c-4cc2-93e1-fe03bbd1c462","timestamp":"2026-09-17T21:18:31.641Z"}
