 two more targeted knob candidates then converge.\"}","originalFile":"{\"candidate_id\": \"c001\", \"parent\": null, \"source_sha256\": \"440a75f0da4810d3d3c5c83adc62c95f0f06cc9677b061798df439273e835d62\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T00:00:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"Faithful Option-A 4-kernel SSD (K1 chunk_cumsum, K2 chunk_state, K3 state_passing, K4 chunk_scan); fp32 accumulation everywhere, tl.dot input_precision='ieee' on all matmuls. Correctness-first baseline to lock all five workloads.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"initial implementation\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (strides/masks/cumsum axis/exp-difference order/dtypes/K3 recurrence & K4 Y_diag+Y_off+D algebra re-derived vs reference; all tl.dot tile shapes have K>=16 and M,N>=16)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"passed\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"passed\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"passed\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"passed\": false, \"speedup\": null}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"RUNTIME_ERROR\", \"passed\": false, \"speedup\": null}], \"all_passed\": false, \"geomean_speedup\": null, \"decision\": \"reject\", \"decision_reason\": \"All 5 workloads RUNTIME_ERROR (uniform, non-tolerance). Kernel compile/launch failure, not algebra. Leading suspects: (a) tl.dot(input_precision='ieee') kwarg unsupported on the installed Triton build (older Triton uses allow_tf32=); (b) fp32 tl.dot on large [64,256]/[256,64] tiles overflowing A800 shared memory with default pipelining/num_warps=8. Harness exposes no traceback; could not probe triton version (bash python blocked by sandbox).\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 1, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Next c002: remove input_precision kwarg entirely and feed bf16 operands into every tl.dot (fp32 accumulation kept by default), which both sidesteps the kwarg-compat risk and halves dot-operand SRAM; keep decays/exp/cumsum/accumulators/D-residual in fp32; add explicit num_warps=4, num_stages=2 to bound SRAM. K1-K4 algebra unchanged (verified).\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c002\", \"parent\": \"c001\", \"source_sha256\": \"0b2cf934a0efdafdd94ec935835da2001dcdf74ae85e1eecb4985b5c41686293\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T00:30:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"c001 failed to launch (all RUNTIME_ERROR). Feed bf16 operands into every tl.dot with default fp32 accumulation and drop the input_precision kwarg; pin num_warps=4/num_stages=2. This removes kwarg-compat risk and halves dot-operand SRAM while keeping all decays/exp/cumsum/accumulators/D-residual in fp32. Expect all 5 to pass and a large speedup over the memory-bound reference.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"All tl.dot now consume bf16 operands (Xd cast to bf16 after fp32 decay in K2; M cast to bf16 after fp32 mask/exp in K4; states_o cast to bf16 for C.states dot); removed input_precision='ieee'; num_warps 8->4 on K2/K4, added num_stages=2 to K2/K4.\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (algebra identical to c001; only dot operand dtype + launch params changed)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 10.486993283989488, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.157534, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.9835205078125}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.25298535944318, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.254157, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 8.973383428660055, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.1579655, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 8.939661476559777, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.1593095, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 13.229428499147975, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.209501, \"max_abs\": 2.0, \"max_rel\": 5.1437907218933105}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 10.640015419344408, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 10.776490409560095, \"decision\": \"accept\", \"decision_reason\": \"All 5 workloads PASS; geomean 10.64x over the memory-bound reference. New best and parent. bf16 tl.dot operands confirmed the c001 failure was compilation/launch (kwarg or SRAM), not algebra. Correctness holds comfortably (bf16 operands == reference operand precision; fp32 accumulation).\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 2, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Occupancy pattern as predicted: 64-tile workloads (WL3 L=256 8.97x, WL4 B=1 8.94x) trail the high-parallelism ones (WL2 256-tile 12.25x, WL5 192-tile 13.23x). Next levers: (c003) head-shared G reuse in K4 -- compute C.B^T once per (b,chunk) across all 16 heads (n_groups=1) to cut the diagonal matmul ~16x, and/or expose more grid parallelism for the 64-tile cases.\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c003\", \"parent\": \"c002\", \"source_sha256\": \"e1384b72946ae8af7ec8f631a889e18370d690406d806776f4aaf2be6364e3e8\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T01:00:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"K4 recomputes the head-independent G = C.B^T 16x (once per head). Precompute G once per (b,chunk) in a new kernel Kg (fp32, lower-tri blocks) and load it in K4, cutting diagonal matmul FLOPs ~2x. Expect a speedup, especially on compute-heavy WL2/WL5.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"Added Kg kernel writing G:[B,NC,Q,Q] fp32; K4 loads G tile instead of computing tl.dot(C_i, B_j^T) and no longer loads B in the j-loop. Diagonal numerics preserved bit-for-bit (G fp32 * fp32 L, then bf16 for M.X). Kg grid (B*NC, Q/BLOCK_M).\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (G indexing im/jm over sG_i/sG_j; only lower-tri blocks written & read; algebra identical to c002 diagonal term)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 9.573956507251205, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.1725575, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.9835205078125}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.768247707569275, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.2439005, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 8.092261318581572, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.1751655, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 8.104532353016493, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.1757255, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.956326519163975, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.213917, \"max_abs\": 2.0, \"max_rel\": 5.1437907218933105}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 10.076294, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 10.299065, \"decision\": \"reject\", \"decision_reason\": \"5/5 PASS but geomean 10.08x REGRESSES vs c002's 10.64x (-5.3%). Splitting G into a separate kernel added a full [B,NC,Q,Q] fp32 DRAM round-trip (write in Kg + read in K4) plus an extra launch, which outweighs the saved matmul FLOPs. Compute-heavy WL2 improved (12.25->12.77) but WL5 dropped (13.23->12.96) and the 64-tile WL1/WL3/WL4 all got worse (10.49->9.57, 8.97->8.09, 8.94->8.10). Confirms c002 is NOT bound by redundant G matmul; the G recompute is essentially free relative to memory/launch overhead. Revert to c002.\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 3, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Lesson: materializing G to DRAM is a net loss; head-shared reuse only helps if kept in-SRAM (loop heads inside one K4 block so G stays resident). Reverted solution.py to c002 source.\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c004\", \"parent\": \"c002\", \"source_sha256\": \"8604925adcacd4247f245ebe23f97fdf253bf2049b546e05bf44e1782973a955\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T01:30:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"c002 per-workload timing suggests a large fixed overhead dominates the small (64-tile) cases (all ~0.158ms regardless of NC/B). Remove kernel K1 (chunk_cumsum) and its acum/alast DRAM tensors by recomputing a=cumsum(A) inline inside K2/K3/K4; expect lower launch+DRAM overhead, especially on WL1/3/4.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"Deleted K1 and the acum:[B,H,NC,Q]/alast:[B,H,NC] tensors. K2 computes acum=cumsum(A), alast=sum(A) inline; K3 computes g=exp(sum(A)) per chunk inline; K4 computes a_full=cumsum(A) over the full Q and extracts a_i/a_j sub-blocks via one-hot mask-sum gathers. Algebra numerically identical to c002.\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (cumsum axis/padding/exp-order identical to c002; one-hot gather a_i=sum(where(q==im,a_full)) exact)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 7.912875213740715, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.208781}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 7.996020196859037, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.3894665}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 7.8974903265166265, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.1794855}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 7.786195396643158, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.18291}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 6.769548762452112, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.4094185}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 7.65801943560213, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 7.6724259792423295, \"decision\": \"reject\", \"decision_reason\": \"5/5 PASS but geomean 7.66x REGRESSES vs c002's 10.64x (-28%). Removing K1 was a net loss: the inline recompute cost (a full Q=256 cumsum PLUS a [BLOCK_M,Q]=[64,256] one-hot gather per i-block in K4, repeated across all B*NC*H*(Q/BLOCK_M) programs) vastly exceeds the single cheap K1 launch + tiny acum/alast round-trip it replaced. Compute-heavy WL2/WL5 collapsed (12.25->8.00, 13.23->6.77) because K4 now redoes the cumsum+gather work per i-block; even the 64-tile cases got worse (K1 was NOT the fixed-overhead culprit). Revert to c002.\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 4, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Lessons so far: (c003) materializing G to DRAM hurts; (c004) inline cumsum recompute hurts and K1 is cheap. Both point to c002 being memory/launch-tuned already, NOT matmul- or K1-bound. The real large tensors are states/states_out:[B,NC,H,P,N] fp32 (~268MB each for WL2, ~1GB round-trip). Next c005: keep c002 structure but store states & states_out in bf16 to halve that traffic. Low risk numerically: K4 already casts states_out->bf16 before the C.states dot (so the off-diagonal term is bit-identical), and K3 accumulates the recurrence in fp32 with only the per-chunk increment rounded to bf16 (NC<=4 additions, well within rtol=0.05). Reverting solution.py to c002 for the c005 branch.\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c005\", \"parent\": \"c002\", \"source_sha256\": \"4dbc6b46eeef0a575fb3892dedbf3585948fbf7ccf44cb0940e3ae507170ee6b\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T02:00:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"c002 is memory/launch-lean but still round-trips the large states/states_out:[B,NC,H,P,N] fp32 tensors (~0.27GB each on WL2). Store both in bf16 to halve that DRAM traffic; compute stays fp32. K4 already cast states_out->bf16 before the C.states dot (off-diagonal bit-identical); K3 recurrence stays fp32 in-register with only the per-chunk increment bf16-rounded (NC<=4 adds, <<rtol). Expect a uniform speedup.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"states and states_out tensors allocated bf16 (was fp32); K2 stores states.to(bf16); K3 stores state.to(bf16) for states_out and carries state in fp32 registers; K4 loads states_out as bf16 directly (removed the redundant .to(bf16)). All arithmetic still fp32.\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (only intermediate storage dtype changed; recurrence/decay/exp/accumulators all fp32; off-diagonal term numerically identical to c002)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 11.030339044159867, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.149774, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.9835205078125}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 14.21968448208945, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.219005, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 9.360167197136782, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.151438, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 9.37955584241099, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.151838, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 14.54705944101824, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.190525, \"max_abs\": 2.0, \"max_rel\": 5.1437907218933105}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 11.490600605016208, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 11.707361201, \"decision\": \"accept\", \"decision_reason\": \"5/5 PASS; geomean 11.49x, up from c002's 10.64x (+8%). New best and parent. Every workload improved (WL1 10.49->11.03, WL2 12.25->14.22, WL3 8.97->9.36, WL4 8.94->9.38, WL5 13.23->14.55). Confirms the states/states_out fp32 round-trip was a real bottleneck; bf16 storage halves it with no correctness loss (max_abs/max_rel identical to c002).\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 5, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Compute-heavy WL2/WL5 gained most (~+2 speedup), consistent with them having the largest states tensors. 64-tile WL3/WL4 still ~9.4x, launch/occupancy-limited. Next c006: attack the 64-tile floor by exposing more grid parallelism, OR try the acum tensor in bf16 too. Prefer the occupancy lever for WL3/WL4.\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c006\", \"parent\": \"c005\", \"source_sha256\": \"4071e143bcc2af05d592472d318d3eb119b37edff58512add4c69419e3d3d3d0\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T02:30:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"The ~0.15ms floor on the small workloads is launch/overhead-bound. Fold the tiny K1 cumsum kernel into K2 (K2 computes a=cumsum(A) inline and STORES acum/alast from its nblk==0 program) so K3/K4 still load them from DRAM as in c005 -- removing one launch + K1's A-read without the expensive K4 recompute that sank c004. Expect a uniform speedup led by the small cases.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"Deleted the K1 launch (4 kernels -> 3). K2 now takes A + acum/alast pointers, computes acum=cumsum(A)/alast=sum(A) inline, and stores them when nblk==0. K3/K4 unchanged (still read acum/alast from DRAM). states/states_out remain bf16. All arithmetic fp32; numerics identical to c005.\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (cumsum/padding/exp-order identical to c005; nblk==0 guard stores acum/alast exactly once per (b,chunk,head); N//BLOCK_N=4 programs share the same cumsum so the stored value is well-defined)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 14.382918707149852, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.1148625, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.9835205078125}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 16.796645182168767, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.185405, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.083240985423238, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.11731, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 12.148485249145912, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.1172305, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 17.774048635961368, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.155934, \"max_abs\": 2.0, \"max_rel\": 5.1437907218933105}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 14.451465, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 14.637067, \"decision\": \"accept\", \"decision_reason\": \"5/5 PASS; geomean 14.45x, up from c005's 11.49x (+26%). New best and parent. Every workload improved substantially (WL1 11.03->14.38, WL2 14.22->16.80, WL3 9.36->12.08, WL4 9.38->12.15, WL5 14.55->17.77). Folding K1 into K2 removed a full launch + A-read and the small workloads gained most (WL1 +30%, WL3/WL4 +29%), confirming the floor was launch/overhead-bound. max_abs/max_rel identical to c005 => numerics unchanged. Contrast with c004: recomputing cumsum in K4 was fatal, but computing it once in K2 and storing is a clear win.\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 6, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Now 3 launches (K2,K3,K4). K3 is tiny (grid B*H*4, NC<=4 sequential) and a candidate to fold into K2 or K4, but the inter-chunk recurrence is sequential over chunks so it cannot trivially merge with the per-(b,chunk) K2/K4. Next c007: try folding K3 into K4 via a single (b,h)-serial mega-kernel that loops chunks carrying state in registers (Option C) to drop another launch + the states/states_out round-trip; risk = serializing chunks & lower parallelism on WL3/WL4. Alternatively micro-tune num_warps/BLOCK_N on K2. c006 is a strong best; remaining gains likely smaller.\"}\n{\"candidate_id\": \"c007\", \"parent\": \"c006\", \"source_sha256\": \"215f96fd287cbd2aff4a70d0b7471dc9dc98422f7fb44da67e99547f8962fa37\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T03:00:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"Fuse K2 (chunk_state) and K3 (state_passing) into one kernel KS (grid B,H,N/BLOCK_N) that carries the [P,BLOCK_N] state in fp32 registers and loops chunks: store states_out[c]=carried state, compute contrib via bf16 dot, state=exp(a_last)*state+contrib. Removes the entire 'states' intermediate tensor AND one launch (3->2). acum still persisted for K4. Expect a further speedup; risk is losing NC-fold parallelism in the state stage (bites B=1 WL4 only).\", \"change_from_parent\": \"Deleted K2 and K3, replaced by fused _state_pass_kernel; removed the states:[B,NC,H,P,N] tensor and the alast tensor. Per-chunk contrib now stays fp32 in-register (no bf16 round-trip through states), marginally more accurate. acum persisted by nblk==0 program. K4 unchanged. Launches 3->2.\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (states_out[c] stored BEFORE the chunk-c update = state from chunks<c, matching reference states_with_init recurrence; final_state=state after last chunk; cumsum/decay/exp-order identical to c006; acum stored once per (b,chunk,head))\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 20.246056936972266, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.081599, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.9835205078125}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 19.79042692459233, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.157358, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 17.03073374103399, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.083231, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 17.07498171616291, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.083407, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 22.596723302949762, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785, \"sol_ms\": 0.122654, \"max_abs\": 2.0, \"max_rel\": 5.1437907218933105}], \"all_passed\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 19.234813, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 19.347784, \"decision\": \"accept\", \"decision_reason\": \"5/5 PASS; geomean 19.23x, up from c006's 14.45x (+33%). New best and parent. Every workload jumped (WL1 14.38->20.25, WL2 16.80->19.79, WL3 12.08->17.03, WL4 12.15->17.07, WL5 17.77->22.60). Fusing K2+K3 removed a launch AND the entire states:[B,NC,H,P,N] bf16 round-trip; the feared serialization on WL4 (B=1, NC=4) did NOT hurt -- WL4 still gained +40%, so on A800 the removed round-trip/launch dominates the lost NC parallelism. max_abs/max_rel identical to c006 => numerics preserved (register-fp32 contrib is >= as accurate).\", \"cumulative_evaluations\": 7, \"skill_usage\": {\"KernelWiki\": \"not_used (A800=sm_80 Ampere; skill scope is Blackwell/Hopper)\"}, \"notes\": \"Now 2 launches (KS, K4) and only acum + states_out cross DRAM between them. Cumulative arc: c002 10.64 -> c005 11.49 -> c006 14.45 -> c007 19.23. Remaining ideas: (a) also fuse acum away by having K4 recompute cumsum ONCE per (b,chunk,head) in a cheap form -- but c004 showed per-i-block recompute is fatal, so would need a single-i-block K4 (BLOCK_M=Q=256) to amortize; (b) micro-tune num_warps in {2,8} / BLOCK_N in {128,256} for KS and K4; (c) collapse K4's states_out read by fusing the off-diagonal into KS. Diminishing returns likely; next c008 = try BLOCK_M=128 or num_warps sweep on K4 (single knob).\"}\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":5,"oldLines":3,"newStart":5,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"candidate_id\": \"c005\", \"parent\": \"c002\", \"source_sha256\": \"4dbc6b46eeef0a575fb3892dedbf3585948fbf7ccf44cb0940e3ae507170ee6b\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T02:00:00Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"c002 is memory/launch-lean but still round-trips the large states/states_out:[B,NC,H,P,N] fp32 tensors (~0.27GB each on WL2). Store both in bf16 to halve that DRAM traffic; compute stays fp32. K4 already cast states_out->bf16 before the C.states dot (off-diagonal bit-identical); K3 recurrence stays fp32 in-register with only the per-chunk increment bf16-rounded (NC<=4 adds, <<rtol). Expect a uniform speedup.\", \"change_from_parent\": \"states and states_out tensors allocated bf16 (was fp32); K2 stores states.to(bf16); K3 stores state.to(bf16) for states_out and carries state in fp32 registers; K4 loads states_out as bf16 directly (removed the redundant .to(bf16)). All arithmetic still fp32.\", \"validation\": {\"static_checks\": \"pass (only intermediate storage dtype changed; recurrence/decay/exp/accumulators all fp32; off-diagonal term numerically identical to c002)\", \"harness_stage\": \"feedback\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"4bf2c843-37b5-5bcc-8809-79651bc70a27\", \"B\": 2, \"L\": 293, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 11.030339044159867, \"ref_ms\": 1.652058, \"sol_ms\": 0.149774, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.9835205078125}, {\"uuid\": \"2907639d-da31-514e-aad2-1a90f808b3ad\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 14.21968448208945, \"ref_ms\": 3.114182, \"sol_ms\": 0.219005, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 4.748114585876465}, {\"uuid\": \"a85c5733-0c73-556f-8514-570137ce6445\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 256, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 9.360167197136782, \"ref_ms\": 1.417485, \"sol_ms\": 0.151438, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 10.30731201171875}, {\"uuid\": \"6dc47920-87cb-522b-be70-75445f3d5ec2\", \"B\": 1, \"L\": 1024, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 9.37955584241099, \"ref_ms\": 1.424173, \"sol_ms\": 0.151838, \"max_abs\": 1.0, \"max_rel\": 5.839954853057861}, {\"uuid\": \"5fd24475-8733-54c5-9b2f-b4c6e85c6346\", \"B\": 4, \"L\": 541, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"passed\": true, \"speedup\": 14.54705944101824, \"ref_ms\": 2.7715785