{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-246f11ca-42e3-407e-aae1-b47434f962fa","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"Claude-Opus-4.8","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll assess convergence before deciding. Two K4 micro-tunes regressed (c008 bigger tiles, c009 more warps), establishing that tiny tiles prefer *fewer* resources. But I have **not** yet applied that lesson to the KS kernel, and KS is the one with a genuine occupancy gap: for the B=1 workload (WL4) its grid is only `B·H·(N/BLOCK_N) = 1·16·4 = 64` programs (< 108 SMs), and WL4/WL3 are the laggards. The c009 finding (\"small tiles prefer fewer warps\") points to a specific, untried, low-risk lever: **KS `num_warps` 4→2**, which reduces warps/block → more blocks/SM, potentially helping the occupancy-limited WL4. This is a justified single-knob candidate, not a speculative sweep."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":208146,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":4871,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6fba2c0-4823-4806-9479-4c36036ed424","uuid":"78c0357f-5bf2-4224-b41f-1777fb95a8cd","timestamp":"2026-09-17T21:42:05.687Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-246f11ca-42e3-407e-aae1-b47434f962fa","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"Claude-Opus-4.8","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NLzzyshs9jZmizkWqgCQCQ","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L2-043_mamba_chunk_scan_with_segsum/solution/solution.py","old_string":"\"\"\"L2/043 Mamba-2 chunk scan with segment sum — Triton solution.\n\nCandidate c007: fuse K2 (chunk_state) and K3 (state_passing) into one kernel.\n\nParent c006 (5/5 PASS, geomean 14.45x, 3 launches K2/K3/K4) is the current best. The two\naccepted wins so far were (c005) storing the big state tensors in bf16 and (c006) removing\na launch by folding K1 into K2 — i.e. both DRAM traffic on the state tensors AND launch\ncount are first-order on these tiny (NC<=4) shapes.\n\nThis candidate removes the whole ``states`` intermediate tensor and one more launch by\nfusing the per-chunk state computation and the inter-chunk recurrence into a single kernel\n``_state_pass_kernel`` (grid (B, H, N/BLOCK_N)). Each program carries the [P, BLOCK_N] SSM\nstate in fp32 registers and loops chunks c=0..NC-1:\n    store states_out[c] = carried-in state (bf16)\n    a = cumsum(A[c]);  a_last = sum(A[c]);  decay = exp(a_last - a)\n    contrib[p,n] = sum_t (X[t,p]*decay[t]) * B[t,n]     (bf16 dot, fp32 accumulate)\n    state = exp(a_last) * state + contrib               (fp32 recurrence)\nfinal_state = state (bf16). The cumsum ``acum`` is still persisted (only the nblk==0\nprogram stores it) because K4 needs a_i / a_j. The ``states`` and ``alast`` tensors are\ngone entirely, and the launch count drops 3 -> 2.\n\nNumerics vs c006: identical algebra, and the per-chunk ``contrib`` now stays fp32 in a\nregister instead of being rounded to bf16 in the ``states`` tensor and reloaded — i.e.\nmarginally MORE accurate. The state recurrence, decays and exp args are all fp32 as before.\nTrade-off: the state-compute stage loses the NC factor of grid parallelism (chunks are now\nserialized within a block). NC<=4 so this only bites the B=1 workload (WL4); the removed\nlaunch + states round-trip is expected to more than compensate, and the single-chunk WL3\nis a pure win (NC=1, no serialization).\n\nStages (compute fp32; states_out/output/final_state stored bf16):\n  KS state+pass     : fused K2+K3 as above (stores acum for K4, states_out, final_state).\n  K4 chunk_scan     : Y = (G∘L)·X + exp(a[t])·(C·states_out) + D[h]·X (reads acum).\n\nConstants for this task: H=16, P=64, N=256, n_groups=1, Q(chunk)=256.\nNo Torch/CPU/NumPy computational fallback anywhere: Triton is the only compute path.\n\"\"\"","new_string":"\"\"\"L2/043 Mamba-2 chunk scan with segment sum — Triton solution.\n\nCandidate c010: KS num_warps 4 -> 2 (single knob vs c007).\n\nParent c007 (5/5 PASS, geomean 19.23x, 2 launches KS/K4) is the current best. The K4\nmicro-tunes both regressed (c008 BLOCK_M=128 -23%, c009 num_warps=8 -23%), teaching that\nthese tiny tiles prefer FEWER resources per block. This candidate applies that lesson to\nthe other kernel: KS ``num_warps`` 4 -> 2.\n\nRationale: KS does small [64,64]-output dots (K=256) plus a Q=256 cumsum and elementwise\nwork, one program per (b, head, n-block). For the B=1 workload (WL4) its grid is only\nB·H·(N/BLOCK_N) = 1·16·4 = 64 programs (< 108 SMs), so KS is occupancy-limited there.\nHalving warps/block frees SM resources for more concurrent blocks, which should lift the\nlow-occupancy WL4/WL3 without hurting the well-populated B=4 workloads. K4 is untouched\n(BLOCK_M=64, num_warps=4). Single knob vs c007.\n\nThis candidate branches from c007; if it does not beat 19.23x it is rejected and c007 is\nkept as the best valid candidate.\n\nStages (compute fp32; states_out/output/final_state stored bf16):\n  KS state+pass     : fused chunk_state + inter-chunk recurrence, state carried in fp32\n                      registers; stores acum for K4, states_out, final_state (num_warps=2).\n  K4 chunk_scan     : Y = (G∘L)·X + exp(a[t])·(C·states_out) + D[h]·X (reads acum),\n                      BLOCK_M=64, num_warps=4.\n\nConstants for this task: H=16, P=64, N=256, n_groups=1, Q(chunk)=256.\nNo Torch/CPU/NumPy computational fallback anywhere: Triton is the only compute path.\n\"\"\""}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":208146,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":4871,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6fba2c0-4823-4806-9479-4c36036ed424","uuid":"8577cf26-ca52-41b2-a19d-b107d5a5b316","timestamp":"2026-09-17T21:42:05.699Z"}
